@page "/"
@implements IAsyncDisposable
@using Backlog.Infrastructure.FileSystem
@using Backlog.Infrastructure.Sync
@using Backlog.Modules.Inbox.Abstractions.DataTransferObjects
@using Backlog.SharedKernel
@using Backlog.SharedKernel.Ai
@using Backlog.Modules.Devbook.Abstractions
@inject TasksDesktopState State
@inject InboxDesktopState InboxState
@inject IServiceProvider Services
@inject IToastChannel Toasts
@inject IJSRuntime JS
@inject IAppFeatureSettings Features
@inject IAzureFoundryChatClient AzureFoundryChat
@* Every area's answer to "what is your content", collected rather than named:
   the shell picks one by which area is open and never learns what a task, an
   item or a chapter looks like. Empty in a host that composes none, and the
   panel is then simply not offered. *@
@inject IEnumerable<IAiContentSource> AiSources
@inject DevbookScope Devbook
@* The surfaces modules register (local ADR 0040 §1), and this window's way of
   being asked to show one (§3). *@
@inject ISurfaceCatalog Catalog
@inject ISurfaceNavigator Navigator
@inject ShellNavigationStore ShellNavigation

@*
    This page. It owns the header and the AI panel, and decides which surface sits
    below them.

    It draws itself again for its own events, and for a Tasks or Inbox change only
    when that change moved something it draws — see HandleStateChanged. Every
    surface below it that reads a state listens to that state itself.

    It used to be called the shell, and it owned the update and feedback windows
    too. Those were never about this page — MainLayout mounts them below every
    route now, in AppFooter — and what is left here is genuinely this screen's:
    the repository scope, the view switch, the Inbox and Devbook toggles, and the
    surfaces they choose between. The `.shell` class on the wrapper is the older name and stays,
    because it is what every layout rule in app.css is written against.

    There are seven, and exactly one of them renders: the workspace — the main view
    the view switch picks, Tasks or Roadmap — the tools surface, the dashboard, the
    session list, the pull requests list, the Inbox and the Devbook. The Inbox and
    then the Devbook took the screen when each outgrew a side pane (local ADR 0022,
    amendments of 2026-10-12 and 2026-10-15); the four before them are takeovers —
    Dev PC Management, the Dashboard and
    Sessions are whole domains, and opening one means the reader has stopped
    looking at the backlog, so it takes the screen and hides every other domain
    concern rather than docking beside them. The roadmap was one of them until it
    became a view. Whichever branch renders owns the page's single <main> landmark.

    The header follows the surface. Every control in it acts on something, and a
    control whose something is off screen is a dead button: the GitHub check
    refreshes the task list, and Ask AI answers from the content of an open area —
    so each of them renders only while what it acts on does. The view switch, the
    Inbox and Devbook toggles, the takeover groups and the identity region stay,
    because they are true of the screen whatever is on it; during a takeover the
    view switch is the way back, since pressing a view closes the takeover and
    shows the workspace.

    What is inside a surface is not the shell's business: Inbox, Backlog
    Management, Devbook, Roadmap, Dev PC Management, Sessions and the
    Dashboard each render themselves from their own project, and the only thing
    that crosses between them here is the repository scope handed to the knowledge
    pane and the dashboard, and the triaged item handed back from the inbox.
*@

@* Read as this render begins, so a change that lands after it is compared with
   what this render drew — see HandleStateChanged. *@
@{ _drawnShellInputs = ReadShellInputs(); }

<div class="shell shell--wide">
    @* The header is four regions rather than one row of controls: who you are and
       over which repositories, what you are looking at, how the workspace is
       doing, and the things that cut across all of it.

       They used to be one flat strip of near-identical pills, which made a
       multi-select, an exclusive view switch, a panel disclosure and two modal
       utilities look like the same kind of control. Grouping them is the whole
       change — almost every behaviour below is the behaviour that was already
       here.

       One row at every width. When the row runs out of room the navigation
       options give up their words before anything wraps, in tiers — the
       Dashboard first, then the work in progress, then the Inbox, then the view
       switch — and keep their glyphs. backlogHeaderFit in app.js measures the row
       and picks the fewest tiers that fit; the word stays in the button as a
       visually hidden span, so the option's name does not change, and its title
       says what it does on hover. Only when every tier is folded and the row still does
       not fit does the header wrap. *@
    <header class="app-header" @ref="_header">
        @* Region A. It used to open with a "Backlog" wordmark, set smaller than the
           other screens' `.app-header h1` to say it was chrome rather than a page
           title. The window border says the name already, so the wordmark was the
           app telling you twice which app you were in and spending the width of the
           word to do it — the region now opens on the repository scope, which is
           what the identity region was actually for. *@
        <div class="app-header__identity">
            @* The scope renders always, with the Brain chip alone when no repository is
               configured (local ADR 0027): the Brain depends on no machine and no
               repository, so a person with only the Brain still has a working header. *@
            @{
                @* One repository on a plain press, one more with Ctrl held — while the
                   task list, the dashboard or the session list is on screen; see ScopeRepository below.
                   The anchor — the first one taken, the one Devbook reads — is marked only
                   once a second chip is pressed: with one chip there is nothing for it
                   to be distinct from, and the markup stays exactly what the single
                   select produced.

                   Fused, because the repositories are siblings read as one control,
                   several of which can be on at once — see Region B for what each
                   shape says. The scope used to be loose chips, which said "related
                   only by kind" of options that are one set. The shared ButtonGroup
                   wears the same header-group shape the view switch does; the chips
                   inside keep their chip
                   classes, because the active fill, the identity edge and the anchor
                   mark all key on those. *@
                <ButtonGroup CssClass="header-group app-header__repository-scope" AriaLabel="Repository scope">
                    @* The Brain leads the scope, first and permanent: the scope of
                       everything filed against no repository (local ADR 0027). It presses
                       as a repository chip does, Ctrl included, and the anchor rules are
                       the repositories' own. It wears band 2 as its identity edge whether
                       or not the Colors switch is on, because it marks a place that is
                       always there rather than a hue somebody chose
                       (.devbook/design/color-scheme.md#the-brains-accent); the word and the
                       first place carry the meaning, never the colour alone. No remove
                       control: there is nothing to remove it from. *@
                    @{
                        var brainSelected = State.BrainInScope;
                        var brainAnchor = brainSelected && State.SelectedRepositoryAliases.Count > 1 && State.BrainIsAnchor;
                    }
                    <ToggleButton BaseClass="chip chip--scope"
                                  CssClass="@BrainScopeChipClass(brainAnchor)"
                                  PressedCssClass="chip--active"
                                  Pressed="brainSelected"
                                  TestId="brain-scope-option"
                                  Title="@BrainScopeChipTitle(brainSelected, brainAnchor)"
                                  aria-current="@(brainAnchor ? "true" : null)"
                                  OnClick="ScopeBrain">
                        <BrainIcon CssClass="chip__icon" />@BrainChipLabel
                    </ToggleButton>
                    @* The divider that sets the Brain off from the repositories, inside
                       the same border: one fused set, its leading member apart. Only
                       while there is a repository to set it off from. *@
                    @if (State.Repositories.Count > 0)
                    {
                        <span class="app-header__scope-divider" aria-hidden="true" data-testid="brain-scope-divider"></span>
                    }
                    @foreach (var repository in State.Repositories)
                    {
                        var selected = State.SelectedRepositoryAliases.Contains(repository.Alias, StringComparer.Ordinal);
                        var anchor = selected && State.SelectedRepositoryAliases.Count > 1
                            && string.Equals(State.AnchorRepositoryAlias, repository.Alias, StringComparison.Ordinal);
                        @* An archived repository is only here while show archived is on,
                           and then says so in words, not by a shade alone (local ADR 0029). *@
                        <ToggleButton BaseClass="chip chip--scope"
                                      CssClass="@RepositoryScopeChipClass(repository, anchor)"
                                      PressedCssClass="chip--active"
                                      Pressed="selected"
                                      TestId="repository-filter-option"
                                      Title="@RepositoryScopeChipTitle(repository, selected, anchor)"
                                      aria-current="@(anchor ? "true" : null)"
                                      data-archived="@(repository.Archived ? "true" : null)"
                                      OnClick="args => ScopeRepository(repository.Alias, args)">
                            @repository.Alias
                            @if (repository.Archived)
                            {
                                <span class="app-header__scope-archived">@ArchivedRepositoryLabel.Suffix</span>
                            }
                        </ToggleButton>
                    }
                </ButtonGroup>

                @* Whether the identity hues are drawn at all — here, beside the chips
                   that wear the most visible ones, rather than buried in Settings with
                   the choice of which hue each repository gets. Those are two different
                   questions asked by two different people at two different moments: one
                   is "this project is green", set once and left alone, and this one is
                   "not right now", which is a decision about the screen in front of you
                   and belongs on it.

                   Outside the scope group on purpose. Inside it this would read as a
                   sixth repository, and it is not a scope — using it narrows nothing.

                   A switch rather than a chip, and the difference is what it is about.
                   Every other control in this strip answers "which of these am I looking
                   at" and holds a selection; this one answers "is this layer on", which
                   is the one question a switch is for. So the shared Toggle in its
                   default shape: role="switch" and aria-checked on a real button, with
                   the name written beside the track instead of on it — which is also why
                   there is no aria-label here. Toggle labels itself from that text
                   through aria-labelledby, and a second accessible name would displace
                   the one the reader can actually see.

                   It renders with the chips or not at all. With nothing configured there
                   is no repository to be an identity for, and a control governing the
                   look of an empty set is a control with nothing to do — the same reason
                   the takeover group below hides itself when no takeover is
                   offered. The Brain's edge is not one of those hues, so it does not
                   keep the switch on screen. *@
                if (State.Repositories.Count > 0)
                {
                    <Toggle CssClass="header-switch"
                            LabelCssClass="header-switch__label"
                            ControlCssClass="toggle__track header-switch__track"
                            Label="Colors"
                            Id="repository-colours-switch"
                            TestId="repository-colours-toggle"
                            Checked="State.RepositoryColoursVisible"
                            CheckedChanged="ShowRepositoryColours" />
                }

                @* Show archived (local ADR 0029, §3): whether the archived repositories
                   are offered in the scope, the Board's columns and the pickers, and
                   their tasks in the list. It asks the question Colors asks — "is this
                   layer on" — and narrows nothing itself, so it is outside the scope
                   group for the same reason.

                   A glyph that stays pressed rather than a second switch, and the
                   reason is room. Colors is the setting everyone meets; this one only
                   exists once a repository is archived, and a track plus its words
                   beside Colors cost the header more width than an occasional choice
                   earns. One on-or-off of its own is the loose standalone option the
                   Inbox and Devbook toggles already wear, so it takes that shape and
                   that size. With no word on screen the name goes on aria-label, and
                   the tooltip says what the next press does; aria-pressed carries the
                   state a switch's aria-checked carried.

                   Kept per device, and read from that choice on every render, so a
                   shell the Router built afresh draws what was left. Only while a
                   repository is archived: with none there is nothing for it to show. *@
                if (State.HasArchivedRepositories)
                {
                    <ToggleButton BaseClass="header-group__option header-group__option--loose header-group__option--icon-only"
                                  PressedCssClass="header-group__option--selected"
                                  Pressed="State.ShowArchivedRepositories"
                                  PressedChanged="State.SetShowArchivedRepositories"
                                  TestId="show-archived-toggle"
                                  AriaLabel="Show archived repositories"
                                  Title="@(State.ShowArchivedRepositories ? "Hide archived repositories" : "Show archived repositories")">
                        <ArchiveIcon />
                    </ToggleButton>
                }
            }
        </div>

        @* Region B — navigation, and nothing else. Left to right: the Inbox
           toggle, the view switch, the Devbook toggle, then the takeovers in two
           groups — the work in progress, and the rest.

           The view switch is the one question every screen answers: which main
           view the workspace shows. Exactly one is always showing, so the switch has
           no "off" and pressing the pressed option changes nothing. The Inbox leads
           the row and the Devbook follows the switch, and both are takeovers: each
           takes the whole screen in place of the view — triage reads one item at a
           time and wants the width, and a chapter, its diagrams and the Brain read
           full width too — and pressing it again returns to the view that was
           showing. Escape closes the Devbook as it closes the other takeovers, but
           not the Inbox, whose own keys use Escape to leave triage. The Tasks
           pane toggle that used to sit between them is gone with the "side panes
           only" state it allowed: the task list is the Tasks view now, and no side
           pane is left at all.

           The shapes. A fused group is a set of sibling destinations read as one
           control — the view switch, and the work in progress (In progress,
           Sessions and Pull requests) — and the pressed
           state says which is on. A standalone option is one on-or-off of its own:
           the Inbox and the Devbook. The loose group is the remaining takeovers,
           related to each other only by being takeovers.

           The view options are also the way back from a takeover: pressing one
           closes it and shows the workspace. During a takeover none of them reads
           pressed, because nothing of the workspace is on screen; the view is kept
           underneath and comes back as it was. *@
        <nav class="app-header__nav" aria-label="Workspace views">
            <div class="app-header__workspace" data-testid="workspace-navigation">
                @* Every header item below stands in a header-surface-item beside its ⧉
                   Open in new window button (local ADR 0040 §4): PopOutButton draws it,
                   only where the host opens windows. The wrapper is there either way,
                   so the header's shape does not depend on the host. *@
                @if (InboxPaneOptionVisible)
                {
                    <span class="header-surface-item" data-testid="@($"{InboxSurfaceId}-header-item")">
                    <ToggleButton BaseClass="header-group__option header-group__option--loose"
                                  PressedCssClass="header-group__option--selected"
                                  Pressed="InboxVisible"
                                  id="inbox-pane-option"
                                  TestId="inbox-pane-option"
                                  aria-controls="inbox-surface"
                                  Title="@InboxOptionTitle"
                                  OnClick="ToggleInbox">
                        <InboxIcon />
                        <span class="header-nav-label header-nav-label--tier-3">Inbox</span>
                        <FeatureStatusBadge Status="@FeatureStatusSlug(AppFeatures.InboxPane)"
                                            Title="@FeatureStatusTitle(AppFeatures.InboxPane)"
                                            TestId="inbox-feature-status" />
                    </ToggleButton>
                    @PopOutButton(InboxSurfaceId)
                    </span>
                }

                @* The view switch. Tasks, Board and Calendar are always offered; the
                   Roadmap while its flag is on. Tasks' option is still the way back
                   from a takeover. In progress is a view too, but its option is the
                   first of the work in progress group further along, not one of these.
                   Each option, and each of that group's, leads with a glyph from
                   the library's Icons folder: decorative, hidden from assistive
                   technology by the glyph itself, so every option's name is still
                   its word (typography-and-layout.md#iconography). *@
                <ButtonGroup CssClass="header-group header-group--views"
                             AriaLabel="View"
                             TestId="view-switch">
                    <span class="header-surface-item" data-testid="@($"{TasksViewId}-header-item")">
                    <ToggleButton BaseClass="header-group__option"
                                  PressedCssClass="header-group__option--selected"
                                  Pressed="ViewOptionPressed(TasksViewId)"
                                  id="tasks-view-option"
                                  Title="Tasks"
                                  TestId="tasks-view-option"
                                  aria-controls="backlog-pane"
                                  OnClick="() => ShowView(TasksViewId)">
                        <ListIcon />
                        <span class="header-nav-label header-nav-label--tier-4">Tasks</span>
                    </ToggleButton>
                    @PopOutButton(TasksViewId)
                    </span>

                    @* The Board: the Tasks pane's filtered rows in columns of cards,
                       right after Tasks because it is the same rows read another way. *@
                    <span class="header-surface-item" data-testid="@($"{BoardViewId}-header-item")">
                    <ToggleButton BaseClass="header-group__option"
                                  PressedCssClass="header-group__option--selected"
                                  Pressed="ViewOptionPressed(BoardViewId)"
                                  id="board-view-option"
                                  Title="Board"
                                  TestId="board-view-option"
                                  aria-controls="backlog-pane"
                                  OnClick="() => ShowView(BoardViewId)">
                        <Columns3Icon />
                        <span class="header-nav-label header-nav-label--tier-4">Board</span>
                    </ToggleButton>
                    @PopOutButton(BoardViewId)
                    </span>

                    @* The Calendar: the Tasks pane's filtered rows on a month by due
                       date. Board sits between it and Tasks. *@
                    <span class="header-surface-item" data-testid="@($"{CalendarViewId}-header-item")">
                    <ToggleButton BaseClass="header-group__option"
                                  PressedCssClass="header-group__option--selected"
                                  Pressed="ViewOptionPressed(CalendarViewId)"
                                  id="calendar-view-option"
                                  Title="Calendar"
                                  TestId="calendar-view-option"
                                  aria-controls="backlog-pane"
                                  OnClick="() => ShowView(CalendarViewId)">
                        <CalendarIcon />
                        <span class="header-nav-label header-nav-label--tier-4">Calendar</span>
                    </ToggleButton>
                    @PopOutButton(CalendarViewId)
                    </span>

                    @if (RoadmapViewOffered)
                    {
                        <span class="header-surface-item" data-testid="@($"{RoadmapViewId}-header-item")">
                        <ToggleButton BaseClass="header-group__option"
                                      PressedCssClass="header-group__option--selected"
                                      Pressed="ViewOptionPressed(RoadmapViewId)"
                                      id="roadmap-view-option"
                                      Title="Roadmap"
                                      TestId="roadmap-view-option"
                                      aria-controls="roadmap-view"
                                      OnClick="() => ShowView(RoadmapViewId)">
                            <ChartGanttIcon />
                            <span class="header-nav-label header-nav-label--tier-4">Roadmap</span>
                            <FeatureStatusBadge Status="@FeatureStatusSlug(RoadmapFeatures.Roadmap)"
                                                Title="@FeatureStatusTitle(RoadmapFeatures.Roadmap)"
                                                TestId="roadmap-feature-status" />
                        </ToggleButton>
                        @PopOutButton(RoadmapViewId)
                        </span>
                    }
                </ButtonGroup>

                @* The registered takeovers, from the surface catalog (local ADR 0040
                   §1): today the Devbook, which follows the switch. Each option's id,
                   test id and the surface it controls are its surface id's, so the
                   Devbook keeps the ids selectors and scenarios find it by. Named for
                   what the Devbook shows, open or not: the Brain's name and its
                   accent while it reads the Brain, Devbook otherwise. *@
                @foreach (var surface in RegisteredTakeoversOffered)
                {
                    var surfaceId = surface.Id;
                    @* Each pops out like every other header item (local ADR 0040 §4):
                       a ⧉ button beside its option, shown on hover and on keyboard
                       focus, and every press opens one more window. *@
                    <span class="header-surface-item" data-testid="@($"{surfaceId}-header-item")">
                    <ToggleButton BaseClass="header-group__option header-group__option--loose"
                                  PressedCssClass="header-group__option--selected"
                                  CssClass="@SurfaceOptionCssClass(surface)"
                                  Pressed="@(RegisteredSurfaceOnScreen?.Id == surfaceId)"
                                  id="@($"{surfaceId}-pane-option")"
                                  TestId="@($"{surfaceId}-pane-option")"
                                  aria-controls="@($"{surfaceId}-surface")"
                                  Title="@SurfaceOptionTitle(surface)"
                                  OnClick="() => ToggleRegisteredSurface(surfaceId)">
                        @SurfaceOptionName(surface)
                        @if (surface.FeatureKey is { } featureKey)
                        {
                            <FeatureStatusBadge Status="@FeatureStatusSlug(featureKey)"
                                                Title="@FeatureStatusTitle(featureKey)"
                                                TestId="@($"{surfaceId}-feature-status")" />
                        }
                    </ToggleButton>
                    @PopOutButton(surfaceId)
                    </span>
                }
            </div>

            @* The work in progress: the goals, and what the assistants are running
               and what they shipped. Goals leads, always offered (local ADR 0035
               §10); then two takeovers of the Sessions context, each under its own
               flag, fused because they are one context's two lists. Each list is a
               takeover, so pressing the pressed one, Escape, or the surface's own
               close button returns to the workspace. *@
            <ButtonGroup CssClass="header-group header-group--work"
                         AriaLabel="Work in progress"
                         TestId="work-in-progress-switcher">
                @* A main view, not a takeover, though it sits with two: pressing
                   it shows the view as a view-switch option does, and nothing
                   closes it. First, because it is what the work is for. *@
                <span class="header-surface-item" data-testid="@($"{GoalsViewId}-header-item")">
                <ToggleButton BaseClass="header-group__option"
                              PressedCssClass="header-group__option--selected"
                              Pressed="ViewOptionPressed(GoalsViewId)"
                              id="goals-view-option"
                              Title="Goals"
                              TestId="goals-view-option"
                              aria-controls="goals-view"
                              OnClick="() => ShowView(GoalsViewId)">
                    <TargetIcon />
                    <span class="header-nav-label header-nav-label--tier-2">Goals</span>
                </ToggleButton>
                @PopOutButton(GoalsViewId)
                </span>

                @if (SessionsOffered)
                {
                    <span class="header-surface-item" data-testid="@($"{SessionsSurfaceId}-header-item")">
                    <ToggleButton BaseClass="header-group__option"
                                  PressedCssClass="header-group__option--selected"
                                  Pressed="SessionsVisible"
                                  id="sessions-toggle-button"
                                  Title="Sessions"
                                  TestId="sessions-toggle-button"
                                  aria-controls="sessions-surface"
                                  PressedChanged="ToggleSessions">
                        <SquareTerminalIcon />
                        <span class="header-nav-label header-nav-label--tier-2">Sessions</span>
                        <FeatureStatusBadge Status="@FeatureStatusSlug(SessionFeatures.Sessions)"
                                            Title="@FeatureStatusTitle(SessionFeatures.Sessions)"
                                            TestId="sessions-feature-status" />
                    </ToggleButton>
                    @PopOutButton(SessionsSurfaceId)
                    </span>
                }

                @* Its own option and its own flag, since it asks GitHub on every
                   opening and the session list asks nothing. *@
                @if (PullRequestsOffered)
                {
                    <span class="header-surface-item" data-testid="@($"{PullRequestsSurfaceId}-header-item")">
                    <ToggleButton BaseClass="header-group__option"
                                  PressedCssClass="header-group__option--selected"
                                  Pressed="PullRequestsVisible"
                                  id="pull-requests-toggle-button"
                                  Title="Pull requests"
                                  TestId="pull-requests-toggle-button"
                                  aria-controls="pull-requests-surface"
                                  PressedChanged="TogglePullRequests">
                        <GitPullRequestIcon />
                        <span class="header-nav-label header-nav-label--tier-2">Pull requests</span>
                        <FeatureStatusBadge Status="@FeatureStatusSlug(SessionFeatures.PullRequests)"
                                            Title="@FeatureStatusTitle(SessionFeatures.PullRequests)"
                                            TestId="pull-requests-feature-status" />
                    </ToggleButton>
                    @PopOutButton(PullRequestsSurfaceId)
                    </span>
                }
            </ButtonGroup>

            @* The remaining takeovers, loose: the Dashboard and Dev PC Management's
               Tools, related only by each taking the screen. The takeover on screen is
               one field because a takeover cannot coexist with the workspace or
               with another takeover, and a group of pressed states describes that
               exclusivity where independent aria-expanded buttons did not. Ask AI
               keeps aria-expanded further down, because that one really is a
               disclosure. Renders only while one of them is offered. *@
            @if (SurfaceSwitcherVisible)
            {
                <ButtonGroup CssClass="header-group header-group--loose header-group--surface"
                             AriaLabel="Surface"
                             TestId="workspace-surface-switcher">
                    @if (DashboardOffered)
                    {
                        <span class="header-surface-item" data-testid="@($"{DashboardSurfaceId}-header-item")">
                        <ToggleButton BaseClass="header-group__option header-group__option--loose"
                                      PressedCssClass="header-group__option--selected"
                                      Pressed="DashboardVisible"
                                      id="dashboard-toggle-button"
                                      Title="Dashboard"
                                      TestId="dashboard-toggle-button"
                                      aria-controls="dashboard-surface"
                                      PressedChanged="ToggleDashboard">
                            <LayoutDashboardIcon />
                            <span class="header-nav-label header-nav-label--tier-1">Dashboard</span>
                            <FeatureStatusBadge Status="@FeatureStatusSlug(DashboardFeatures.Dashboard)"
                                                Title="@FeatureStatusTitle(DashboardFeatures.Dashboard)"
                                                TestId="dashboard-feature-status" />
                        </ToggleButton>
                        @PopOutButton(DashboardSurfaceId)
                        </span>
                    }

                    @* The notification centre, with the count of questions waiting
                       for an answer on the option itself: the one number in the
                       header that asks the person for something, so it rides the
                       control that answers it rather than a status region. *@
                    @if (NotificationsOffered)
                    {
                        <span class="header-surface-item" data-testid="@($"{NotificationsSurfaceId}-header-item")">
                        <ToggleButton BaseClass="header-group__option header-group__option--loose"
                                      PressedCssClass="header-group__option--selected"
                                      Pressed="NotificationsVisible"
                                      id="notifications-toggle-button"
                                      Title="Notifications"
                                      TestId="notifications-toggle-button"
                                      aria-controls="notifications-surface"
                                      PressedChanged="ToggleNotifications">
                            <BellIcon />
                            <span class="header-nav-label header-nav-label--tier-1">Notifications</span>
                            <NotificationsWaitingBadge />
                            <FeatureStatusBadge Status="@FeatureStatusSlug(NotificationsFeatures.Notifications)"
                                                Title="@FeatureStatusTitle(NotificationsFeatures.Notifications)"
                                                TestId="notifications-feature-status" />
                        </ToggleButton>
                        @PopOutButton(NotificationsSurfaceId)
                        </span>
                    }

                    @if (ToolsOffered)
                    {
                        <span class="header-surface-item" data-testid="@($"{ToolsSurfaceId}-header-item")">
                        <ToggleButton BaseClass="header-group__option header-group__option--loose"
                                      PressedCssClass="header-group__option--selected"
                                      Pressed="ToolsVisible"
                                      id="tools-toggle-button"
                                      TestId="tools-toggle-button"
                                      aria-controls="tools-surface"
                                      PressedChanged="ToggleTools">
                            Tools
                            <FeatureStatusBadge Status="@FeatureStatusSlug(DevPcFeatures.SystemTools)"
                                                Title="@FeatureStatusTitle(DevPcFeatures.SystemTools)"
                                                TestId="tools-feature-status" />
                        </ToggleButton>
                        @PopOutButton(ToolsSurfaceId)
                        </span>
                    }
                </ButtonGroup>
            }
        </nav>

        @* Region C — how the workspace is doing. Ambient rather than actionable,
           which is why it is the quietest thing in the row and why it no longer
           sits in the middle of the navigation. The auto margin lives here now, so
           the status and the utilities travel to the right edge together.

           The save state used to lead this region and no longer does. It was the
           one thing here that was true of the window rather than of this screen,
           and it was therefore missing from every other route — so it went to the
           band AppFooter mounts under all of them. The sync control stayed, because
           checking GitHub is a thing you do to the list in front of you. *@
        <div class="app-header__status">
            @* Nothing on screen waits on GitHub: the backlog has to open instantly
               and offline. This button re-reads everything, on demand. Beside it
               runs one background poll, CheckPullRequestsAfterLoad's: once the list
               is showing, after every reload, and on the state's recheck tick every
               PullRequestRecheckInterval while the window is open and the GitHub
               integration is on, it asks about pull requests not yet landed. It
               blocks nothing when GitHub cannot be reached.

               Only while the task list is on screen — the list is what a check
               refreshes, and the dashboard fetches from GitHub on its own.

               AppButton rather than IconButton for its Busy: a check takes as long
               as GitHub does, so the glyph gives way to a spinner and the button
               stays focusable and ignores a second press while it runs, rather
               than dropping out of the tab order under the native disabled
               (interaction-guidelines.md#busy-actions). *@
            @if (GitHubSyncOffered)
            {
                <AppButton BaseClass="icon-link"
                           Variant="ButtonVariant.None"
                           IconAlone="true"
                           id="github-sync-button"
                           TestId="github-sync-button"
                           Busy="@State.GitHubSyncing"
                           BusyLabel="Checking GitHub"
                           aria-label="Check GitHub for issue and pull request updates"
                           title="Check GitHub for issue and pull request updates"
                           OnClick="() => State.SyncGitHubAsync()">
                    <Icon>⟳</Icon>
                </AppButton>
            }
        </div>

        @* Region D — the cross-cutting concerns, at the far edge and last in
           reading order: not one of them is about the work on screen.

           Ask AI is the one accented control in the whole header. It is a designed
           first-class surface rather than a utility, and holding the accent back to
           a single control is what lets it mean something instead of becoming the
           header's wallpaper — which is what the old all-gold row had made it. *@
        <div class="app-header__utilities">
            @* Offered while there is content to ask about — while at least one area
               with an Ask AI source behind it is on screen. The panel answers from that
               area's own content, not from what the screen is showing of it, so a
               takeover no longer hides the button: the Dashboard and Tools are areas
               too. What does hide it is a screen with no area at all, which is the one
               case where the button would open nothing about this screen. *@
            @if (AiAssistantOffered)
            {
                @* A disclosure, not a pressed state: this opens a panel beside the
                   content, so it carries aria-expanded and stays AppButton rather
                   than ToggleButton. The takeover group above is the other case —
                   a selection, not an expansion.

                   The state is written out rather than bound to the bool: Blazor
                   renders a true bool attribute as `aria-expanded=""` and omits it
                   entirely when false, and neither is a value aria-expanded accepts.
                   Now that this is the header's only disclosure, it is the one place
                   that has to say "true" or "false" — which is what the two takeover
                   buttons did before they became a selection. *@
                <AppButton BaseClass="header-accent"
                           Variant="ButtonVariant.None"
                           id="ai-toggle-button"
                           TestId="ai-toggle-button"
                           aria-controls="ai-assistant-panel"
                           aria-expanded="@(_aiExpanded ? "true" : "false")"
                           OnClick="ToggleAiPanel">
                    Ask AI
                    <FeatureStatusBadge Status="@FeatureStatusSlug(AppFeatures.AiAssistant)"
                                        Title="@FeatureStatusTitle(AppFeatures.AiAssistant)"
                                        TestId="ai-feature-status" />
                </AppButton>
            }

            @* Two utilities left, and they are the two that are still about this
               screen: Ask AI opens a panel beside its content, and settings is
               where this screen's own configuration lives. The version control
               and the Report issue button were neither, and they are now in the
               app-shell footer MainLayout mounts below every route. *@
            <a class="icon-link" href="settings" id="settings-link" data-testid="settings-link" title="Settings" aria-label="Settings">⚙</a>
        </div>
    </header>

    @* Gated the way its button is: the panel follows the areas it reads from, and
       comes back open if the reader left it open, because _aiExpanded is not
       touched by a takeover. *@
    @if (AiAssistantOffered && _aiExpanded)
    {
        <section class="ai-panel" id="ai-assistant-panel" data-testid="ai-assistant-panel" aria-labelledby="ai-assistant-title">
            @* The library's section header. This one stays a div: the panel is
               already a section named by its heading, and the header it had was
               never a landmark of its own. *@
            <SectionHeader Element="div"
                           BaseClass="ai-panel__header"
                           TextCssClass=""
                           EyebrowCssClass="ai-panel__eyebrow"
                           TitleCssClass=""
                           ActionsCssClass=""
                           Eyebrow="Azure Foundry"
                           TitleId="ai-assistant-title"
                           Title="Ask about this content">
                <Actions>
                    <a class="ai-panel__settings" href="settings">Settings</a>
                </Actions>
            </SectionHeader>
            @* Which area answers: the one on screen, named in the sentence. There
               used to be a row of chips to choose between areas while a side pane
               sat beside the view; one area is on screen at a time now, and a group
               of one is a control with no choice in it. *@
            <p class="ai-panel__body">
                @AiPanelBody
            </p>
            @* The question box and the Ask button, in a component of their own so a
               keystroke draws the box and not this page. The question is still kept
               here, so it outlives the panel closing. *@
            <AskAiQuestion Question="@_aiQuestion"
                           KeepQuestion="KeepAiQuestion"
                           Busy="@_aiBusy"
                           OnAsk="AskAiAsync" />
            @* The failure used to be a line under this box. It is a toast now: the
               panel is a takeover the reader opens over their work, and a refusal
               that stayed pinned inside it was still there next time they opened it.
               See AskAiAsync, where each catch publishes on its way out. *@
            @if (_aiAnswer is not null)
            {
                <article class="ai-panel__answer" data-testid="ai-answer">
                    <h3>Answer</h3>
                    <p>@_aiAnswer</p>
                </article>
            }
        </section>
    }

    @* Exactly one of these renders, and each one's root is the page's only
       <main>. A takeover is deliberately not a Modal: there is no scrim and no
       focus trap, because the header behind it is not inert — it is where the
       control that closes the surface lives. Modal.razor still owns the modal
       semantics for the update and feedback dialogs, which really are modal.

       A takeover is a module's surface, rendered from its catalog entry (local ADR
       0040 §1) with the query this window shows it with: the Inbox, the Devbook,
       the session and pull request lists, the Dashboard, the notification centre and
       Tools. Escape closes it, as the takeovers always closed, except where its
       registration keeps Escape for keys of its own — the Inbox, which uses it to
       leave triage, so it carries no Escape-closes marker and no surface key
       handler, and its header option, or another surface's, is the way back. A key a
       field, a draft's control or a dialog inside a surface handles stays there: the
       library's Combobox and Modal end the key's walk up to this handler, so one
       Escape closes the list or the dialog and leaves the surface on screen. *@
    @if (TakeoverOnScreen is { } shown)
    {
        if (shown.EscapeCloses)
        {
            <main class="workspace-surface"
                  id="@($"{shown.Id}-surface")"
                  data-testid="@($"{shown.Id}-surface")"
                  tabindex="-1"
                  @ref="_surfaceElement"
                  @onkeydown="OnSurfaceKeyDown"
                  data-escape-closes
                  aria-label="@SurfaceOptionName(shown)">
                <DynamicComponent @key="shown.Id" Type="shown.Component" Parameters="SurfaceParameters(shown)" />
            </main>
        }
        else
        {
            <main class="workspace-surface"
                  id="@($"{shown.Id}-surface")"
                  data-testid="@($"{shown.Id}-surface")"
                  aria-label="@SurfaceOptionName(shown)">
                <DynamicComponent @key="shown.Id" Type="shown.Component" Parameters="SurfaceParameters(shown)" />
            </main>
        }
    }
    else
    {
        @* The main view and nothing beside it: the Devbook pane, its draggable edge
           and the layout that split the row with it went when the Devbook took the
           screen. *@
        <main class="workspace" id="workspace" data-testid="workspace">
            @* The main view, rendered from its catalog entry (local ADR 0040 §1) with
               the query this window last asked it with: Tasks, Board, Calendar, the
               Roadmap or Goals, each its module's own surface. A view is replaced and
               never closed, so the close callback it is handed does nothing. *@
            @if (ViewOnScreen is { } view)
            {
                <DynamicComponent Type="view.Component" Parameters="ViewParameters(view)" />
            }
        </main>
    }
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The takeover on screen, by its surface id (local ADR 0040 §2), or null for the
    /// workspace — the main view the view switch picks, alone.
    /// <para>
    /// One field rather than a flag per takeover, and that is the whole point: a
    /// takeover cannot coexist with the workspace, and the takeovers cannot coexist
    /// with each other — opening any of them means the reader has stopped looking at
    /// the backlog. It was the <c>WorkspaceSurface</c> enum, which kept the takeovers
    /// exclusive by construction; the modules register their takeovers now, so the
    /// id of one in the catalog's takeover group keeps them exclusive instead, and a
    /// takeover the catalog does not offer — its flag off, or not registered — is the
    /// workspace. Deliberately a field of its own beside <see cref="_view"/>: closing
    /// a takeover simply reveals the view that was there all along.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private string? _takeover;

    /// <summary>The query the takeover on screen was asked with — a jump's session,
    /// pull request or notification — handed to it until the next takeover is asked
    /// for. The Devbook's is the shell's own; see <see cref="SurfaceQuery"/>.</summary>
    private IReadOnlyDictionary<string, string> _takeoverQuery = NoQuery;

    /// <summary>The main view the workspace shows, by its surface id (local ADR 0040
    /// §2). Kept while a takeover is up, so closing the takeover shows the view the
    /// reader left.</summary>
    private string _view = TasksViewId;

    /// <summary>The query the main view was last asked with — a jump's plan or entry —
    /// handed to it until the next view is asked for. One instance per request: the
    /// view follows a query it has not been handed before, so drawing again with the
    /// same one asks nothing new.</summary>
    private IReadOnlyDictionary<string, string> _viewQuery = NoQuery;

    private static readonly IReadOnlyDictionary<string, string> NoQuery =
        new Dictionary<string, string>(StringComparer.Ordinal);
    private bool _aiExpanded;
    private DotNetObjectReference<Home>? _selfReference;

    /// <summary>The header row backlogHeaderFit folds the navigation's words in.</summary>
    private ElementReference _header;
    private ElementReference _surfaceElement;
    private bool _focusSurfacePending;

    private string _aiQuestion = string.Empty;
    private string? _aiAnswer;
    private bool _aiBusy;

    /// <summary>The sources by area key, taken once: the collection is what a
    /// host composed and does not change while the page is up.</summary>
    private Dictionary<string, IAiContentSource> _aiSources = new(StringComparer.Ordinal);

    /// <summary>The id the refusal carried while it was an inline alert under the
    /// question box. It rides on the toast now, so a selector written against
    /// either surface still finds the sentence.</summary>
    private const string AiErrorTestId = "ai-error";

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        _linkedWork = BuildLinkedWork();
        _shellWindows = Services.GetService<IShellWindows>();

        // Reopen on the surface the reader was last looking at rather than
        // always defaulting to the workspace panes, by its surface id. The store
        // reads a name the retired WorkspaceSurface enum stored as its id, and a file
        // from when the roadmap was a takeover as the Roadmap view over the
        // workspace. An id the catalog does not offer as a takeover — first launch
        // included — leaves the workspace on screen (TakeoverOnScreen).
        _takeover = ShellNavigation.LastSurface;

        // The view the workspace was showing, by its surface id. The store reads a
        // name the retired ShellView enum stored as its id. An id no view registered
        // reads as Tasks (EffectiveView).
        _view = ShellNavigation.LastView;

        // The side panes a file from before the Devbook took the screen had open
        // are read by the store, which turns a Devbook that was on screen into the
        // Devbook surface restored above. That restore is what makes it survive
        // Settings and back, not only a full relaunch: the Router disposes and
        // reconstructs this component on every route change.

        // And the repository scope, anchor first, so the Devbook option — which reads
        // the anchor — is there without pressing a chip. Before the state is
        // listened to, because every change it announces is written back to the file
        // (HandleStateChanged), and the list's first load must not write the empty
        // scope over the remembered one. A repository removed from Settings since is
        // dropped by the state; a plural scope on a screen that shows one is narrowed
        // to the anchor by the pane sync after the load. With the repository registry
        // unreadable this launch cannot tell a removed repository from one it merely
        // failed to read, so the restore waits for a readable registry
        // (RememberRepositoryScope) and nothing is written over the file meanwhile.
        RememberRepositoryScope();

        // First registration wins for a key, so a host that composed two
        // sources for one area gets the one it wrote first rather than a throw
        // on the page's first render.
        _aiSources = AiSources
            .GroupBy(source => source.AreaKey, StringComparer.Ordinal)
            .ToDictionary(group => group.Key, group => group.First(), StringComparer.Ordinal);

        State.Changed += HandleStateChanged;

        // Optional, the way Settings reads it: a host that composes no Brain store
        // shows the chip as not yet set up, which still scopes.
        _brainSettings = Services.GetService<IBrainSettings>();
        if (_brainSettings is not null) _brainSettings.Changed += HandleBrainSettingsChanged;

        // The Brain the Devbook pane reads. Optional for the same reason: a host
        // that composes none offers the pane for repositories only.
        _brainFolders = Services.GetService<IBrainFolderSource>();

        // The notes of the owner's Brain that name tasks, for the rows' Brain
        // badges (local ADR 0026 §3). Read again whenever the Brain announces a
        // change, which is also how a rebuilt database is announced.
        _brainTaskLinks = Services.GetService<IBrainTaskLinks>();
        if (_brainFolders is not null && _brainTaskLinks is not null)
        {
            _brainFolders.Changed += HandleBrainFoldersChanged;
            _ = ReadBrainNotesByTaskAsync();
        }
        State.WrittenElsewhere += HandleTasksWrittenElsewhere;
        State.PullRequestRecheckDue += HandlePullRequestRecheckDue;
        InboxState.Changed += HandleStateChanged;
        InboxState.Routed += HandleInboxRouted;
        InboxState.BatchRouted += HandleInboxBatchRouted;
        InboxState.RouteUndone += HandleInboxRouteUndone;
        Features.Changed += HandleFeaturesChanged;
        Navigator.ShowRequested += HandleSurfaceRequested;
        _gitHubIntegrationWasOn = IsFeatureEnabled(TasksFeatures.GitHubIntegration);

        // Optional, the way Settings resolves it: a host without task sync
        // composes no worker, and a test rendering this page should not have to.
        _taskSync = ResolveTaskSync();
        if (_taskSync is not null) _taskSync.Changed += HandleTaskSyncChanged;

        // Optional on the same terms, and for a second reason: this is the window
        // answering questions from outside, so a host that composed no activator
        // simply is not asked, which is the correct behaviour rather than a gap.
        _surfaceAttachment = Services.GetService<SessionsSurfaceActivator>()?.Attach(DescribeSessionsAsync);

        // A popped-out window's Show in main window (local ADR 0040 §4), on the same
        // optional terms: this window is the main one, so the windows beside it reach
        // its surfaces through here.
        _mainWindowAttachment = Services.GetService<MainWindowSurfaces>()?.Attach(ShowFromAnotherWindowAsync);

        // The notification centre on the same optional terms: an owner desktop composes it, a
        // test rendering this page or a host without the module does not, and then the option
        // is simply not offered. The requests are a Windows notification asking for the centre.
        _notificationCentreComposed = Services.GetService<INotificationCentre>() is not null;
        _notificationRequests = Services.GetService<NotificationCentreRequests>()?.Listen(OpenNotificationCentre);

        // The scenario screenshots an entry's body names, answered for the Tasks
        // pane, which may not reach the scenario pages. A host that composed no
        // scenario store gets no source, and an entry's images render as before.
        _devbookImages = new global::Backlog.Desktop.UI.Devbook.DevbookScenarioImageSources(
            Services.GetService<global::Backlog.Desktop.UI.Devbook.DevbookScenarioStore>());
        _devbookImages.Changed += HandleDevbookImagesChanged;

        // The window's reader of sessions and open pull requests, shared with the
        // Goals view through the window's source; fresh on every mount, so a trip to
        // Settings and back reads again.
        _work = Services.GetService<ShellGoalsViewSource>()?.Renew() ?? WorkInProgressReader.From(Services);

        // What the Tasks views draw beside the list that only this page holds, handed
        // to them through the window's wiring before the first view draws.
        _tasksViewWiring = Services.GetService<TasksViewWiring>();
        WireTasksViews();

        // The Dashboard's Ask AI, which only this window offers: Ask AI stays in the
        // main window (local ADR 0040 §4).
        _dashboardWiring = Services.GetService<Backlog.Modules.Dashboard.UI.DashboardSurfaceWiring>();
        WireDashboard();

        await State.InitializeAsync();
        SynchronizeDevbookAvailability();
        CheckPullRequestsAfterLoad();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Has the Tasks state read, in the background, the entries' pull requests it
    /// does not know to have landed — never read, or last read open and not re-read
    /// for a while — so work that merged shows merged without the sync button,
    /// whatever the entry's status, and a prompt entry whose pull request merged into
    /// the default branch or a <c>plan/</c> branch goes Done. Called after the list
    /// loads, after every reload of it, and on the state's recheck tick
    /// (<see cref="HandlePullRequestRecheckDue"/>), so an idle window still notices.
    /// <para>
    /// Not awaited: the list is already on screen and must stay usable offline, and
    /// the state keeps its own one-at-a-time guard and stays quiet on failure. The
    /// flag is checked here because only the shell knows the feature switches; the
    /// state checks for a configured repository itself.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private void CheckPullRequestsAfterLoad()
    {
        if (!IsFeatureEnabled(TasksFeatures.GitHubIntegration)) return;

        _ = State.CheckPullRequestsAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>The state's recheck tick, raised on a timer thread: the check runs on
    /// this dispatcher, behind the same feature switch as every other.</summary>
    private void HandlePullRequestRecheckDue() => _ = InvokeAsync(CheckPullRequestsAfterLoad);

    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (firstRender)
        {
            _selfReference = DotNetObjectReference.Create(this);

            try
            {
                await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("backlogTakeoverEscape.register", _selfReference);
            }
            catch (Exception)
            {
                // Without it Escape still closes a surface from inside the surface;
                // only the press after focus fell to <body> goes unheard.
            }

            try
            {
                await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("backlogHeaderFit.observe", _header);
            }
            catch (Exception)
            {
                // Without it every option keeps its word and a narrow window
                // overflows the row instead of folding it.
            }
        }

        // The two branches below run on every render, not only the first: a
        // surface can open and close any number of times after it.
        if (WorkspaceVisible)
        {
            _focusSurfacePending = false;
            return;
        }

        // Moving focus onto the surface is what announces the context change and
        // makes Escape work without a click first. Once per open — guarded on the
        // flag rather than on firstRender, because a re-render inside an open
        // surface must not drag focus back out of whatever it is now on.
        if (!_focusSurfacePending) return;

        _focusSurfacePending = false;
        try
        {
            // A takeover that keeps Escape for its own keys takes focus inside
            // itself rather than on the landmark, which it draws without one. The
            // Inbox's keys — j, k, t and Escape out of triage — are heard only while
            // focus is within its pane, so landing on the surface would leave them
            // dead until a click.
            if (TakeoverOnScreen is { EscapeCloses: false } keepsEscape)
            {
                if (keepsEscape.Id == InboxSurfaceId) await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("backlogFocus", InboxPaneId, false);
            }
            else
            {
                await _surfaceElement.FocusAsync();
            }
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // Focus is an improvement on where the browser would have left it,
            // not something the surface depends on.
        }
    }

    /// <summary>The takeovers' surface ids (local ADR 0040 §1). The takeovers are their
    /// modules' surfaces; the shell names their ids, to place each option in the
    /// header and to answer what is on screen, and never their components.</summary>
    private const string InboxSurfaceId = Backlog.Modules.Inbox.UI.InboxSurfaceRegistration.SurfaceId;
    private const string SessionsSurfaceId = Backlog.Modules.Sessions.UI.SessionsSurfaceRegistration.SurfaceId;
    private const string PullRequestsSurfaceId = Backlog.Modules.Sessions.UI.SessionsSurfaceRegistration.PullRequestsSurfaceId;
    private const string DashboardSurfaceId = Backlog.Modules.Dashboard.UI.Extensions.DashboardSurfaceRegistration.SurfaceId;
    private const string NotificationsSurfaceId = NotificationsSurfaceRegistration.SurfaceId;
    private const string ToolsSurfaceId = Backlog.Modules.DevPc.UI.ToolsSurfaceRegistration.SurfaceId;

    /// <summary>The takeovers the header places itself — each in its group, with its
    /// glyph and its fold tier. Any other takeover the catalog offers is drawn after
    /// the view switch, beside the Devbook, without a shell edit.</summary>
    private static readonly HashSet<string> PlacedTakeovers = new(StringComparer.Ordinal)
    {
        InboxSurfaceId, SessionsSurfaceId, PullRequestsSurfaceId, DashboardSurfaceId, NotificationsSurfaceId, ToolsSurfaceId,
    };

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether this window offers a takeover: the catalog offers it in the takeover
    /// group — registered, its flag on — and the shell has something for it to show:
    /// the Devbook only while there is something to read, the notification centre only
    /// where the host composed the owner's centre, so a host without it offers no
    /// option rather than one that opens on nothing.
    /// </summary>
    private bool TakeoverOffered(string surfaceId) =>
        Catalog.TryGet(surfaceId, out var surface)
        && surface.Group == ShellSurfaceGroup.Takeover
        && surfaceId switch
        {
            DevbookSurfaceId => DevbookPaneOptionVisible,
            NotificationsSurfaceId => _notificationCentreComposed,
            _ => true,
        };

    /// <summary>The takeover on screen: the one chosen, while this window offers it —
    /// else none, and the workspace is on screen. Its flag switched off returns the
    /// reader to the workspace; switched back on, the choice shows again.</summary>
    private ShellSurface? TakeoverOnScreen =>
        _takeover is { } id && TakeoverOffered(id) && Catalog.TryGet(id, out var surface) ? surface : null;

    /// <summary>Whether <paramref name="surfaceId"/> is the takeover on screen.</summary>
    private bool TakeoverVisible(string surfaceId) => TakeoverOnScreen?.Id == surfaceId;

    private bool ToolsVisible => TakeoverVisible(ToolsSurfaceId);

    private bool ToolsOffered => TakeoverOffered(ToolsSurfaceId);

    private bool DashboardVisible => TakeoverVisible(DashboardSurfaceId);

    private bool DashboardOffered => TakeoverOffered(DashboardSurfaceId);

    private bool SessionsVisible => TakeoverVisible(SessionsSurfaceId);

    private bool SessionsOffered => TakeoverOffered(SessionsSurfaceId);

    private bool PullRequestsVisible => TakeoverVisible(PullRequestsSurfaceId);

    private bool PullRequestsOffered => TakeoverOffered(PullRequestsSurfaceId);

    private bool NotificationsVisible => TakeoverVisible(NotificationsSurfaceId);

    private bool NotificationsOffered => TakeoverOffered(NotificationsSurfaceId);

    /// <summary>Whether the workspace — the main view — is on screen. Anything that
    /// is not a takeover is the workspace, including a surface whose feature has
    /// been switched off.</summary>
    private bool WorkspaceVisible => TakeoverOnScreen is null;

    /// <summary>The surface ids of the five views (local ADR 0040 §1). The views are
    /// their modules' surfaces; the shell names their ids, to place each option in the
    /// header, and never their components.</summary>
    private const string TasksViewId = TasksSurfaceQuery.SurfaceId;
    private const string BoardViewId = TasksSurfaceQuery.BoardId;
    private const string CalendarViewId = TasksSurfaceQuery.CalendarId;
    private const string RoadmapViewId = RoadmapSurfaceQuery.SurfaceId;
    private const string GoalsViewId = Backlog.Modules.Progress.UI.Goals.GoalsSurfaceQuery.SurfaceId;

    /// <summary>The view the workspace shows: the one chosen, while the catalog offers
    /// it as a view — else Tasks, which is always offered, as for the Roadmap with its
    /// feature switched off. The choice itself is kept, so switching the feature back
    /// on shows it again.</summary>
    private string EffectiveView => ViewOffered(_view) ? _view : TasksViewId;

    /// <summary>The view surface on screen when the workspace is: the effective view's
    /// catalog entry, or none in a host that registered no Tasks view.</summary>
    private ShellSurface? ViewOnScreen =>
        Catalog.TryGet(EffectiveView, out var surface) && surface.Group == ShellSurfaceGroup.View ? surface : null;

    /// <summary>Whether the catalog offers <paramref name="viewId"/> as a view: registered,
    /// in the view group, its feature on.</summary>
    private bool ViewOffered(string viewId) =>
        Catalog.TryGet(viewId, out var surface) && surface.Group == ShellSurfaceGroup.View;

    /// <summary>The effective view's name, for "Back to …".</summary>
    private string EffectiveViewTitle => ViewOnScreen?.Title ?? "Tasks";

    /// <summary>What the main view's component is handed: the query it was last asked
    /// with, and a close callback that does nothing — a view is replaced, never closed.</summary>
    private Dictionary<string, object?> ViewParameters(ShellSurface view) => new(StringComparer.Ordinal)
    {
        [ShellSurface.QueryParameter] = _viewQuery,
        [ShellSurface.CloseParameter] = default(EventCallback),
    };

    /// <summary>Whether the Goals view is on screen. Always offered (local ADR 0035 §10).</summary>
    private bool GoalsViewVisible => WorkspaceVisible && EffectiveView == GoalsViewId;

    /// <summary>Whether the task list is in front of the reader — the Tasks view, or
    /// the Board or the Calendar laying out the same filtered rows, on screen in
    /// the workspace, not merely chosen underneath a takeover. The GitHub check acts
    /// on the list and is offered on this answer; so is the Tasks area of Ask AI.</summary>
    private bool TasksListOnScreen => WorkspaceVisible && EffectiveView is TasksViewId or BoardViewId or CalendarViewId;

    /// <summary>Whether the header offers the GitHub check: the list is on screen,
    /// the integration is on and configured, and a row links to GitHub. Read by the
    /// markup and by <see cref="ReadShellInputs"/>, so the two cannot drift.</summary>
    private bool GitHubSyncOffered => TasksListOnScreen && IsFeatureEnabled(TasksFeatures.GitHubIntegration) && State.GitHubConfigured && State.HasLinkedRows;

    /// <summary>Whether what is on screen can show several repositories at once:
    /// the task list, the Dashboard, the session list, or the pull requests list —
    /// and the Inbox, which shows none but routes into the list: it sat beside the
    /// list before it took the screen, and a triage session must not cost the reader
    /// the scope the list goes back to.
    /// The Devbook too, for the same reason and one of its own: it reads the anchor,
    /// but it sat beside the list until it took the screen (local ADR 0022,
    /// amendment of 2026-10-15), and with the Brain and a repository both pressed it
    /// offers its <c>Brain | repo</c> switch (local ADR 0027, §4), which a scope
    /// narrowed to the anchor would take away. Tools reads none, so there a chip is a
    /// single select and the scope is kept to its anchor.</summary>
    private bool RepositoryScopeTakesSeveral =>
        TasksListOnScreen || GoalsViewVisible || DashboardVisible || SessionsVisible || PullRequestsVisible || InboxVisible || DevbookVisible;

    /// <summary>Offered while the feature is on and there is an area to ask about.
    /// It used to be the task list alone; every area answers now, so the button
    /// follows whichever of them is on screen.</summary>
    private bool AiAssistantOffered => IsFeatureEnabled(AppFeatures.AiAssistant) && OpenAiArea is not null;

    /// <summary>
    /// The area on screen, when a source answers for it; null otherwise.
    /// <para>
    /// One at a time: every area is a view or a surface, and the shell shows one of
    /// each kind's at most, never two. The side panes once put a second area beside
    /// the view and a row of chips chose between them; both went when the Devbook
    /// took the screen. Only the keys a host actually composed a source for count,
    /// so a host without a Devbook source offers no Ask AI on the Devbook.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private IAiContentSource? OpenAiArea
    {
        get
        {
            string? key =
                InboxVisible ? "inbox"
                : TasksListOnScreen ? "tasks"
                : RoadmapViewVisible ? "roadmap"
                : GoalsViewVisible ? "goals"
                : DevbookVisible ? "devbook"
                : DashboardVisible ? "dashboard"
                : SessionsVisible ? "sessions"
                : ToolsVisible ? "tools"
                : null;

            return key is not null && _aiSources.TryGetValue(key, out var source) ? source : null;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>The area a question goes to: the one on screen, or nothing — in
    /// which case the panel is not offered at all.</summary>
    private string? EffectiveAiScope => OpenAiArea?.AreaKey;

    /// <summary>The sentence under the panel's title, naming the area that answers.</summary>
    private string AiPanelBody => $"Answers from the {OpenAiArea?.AreaTitle} content.";

    /// <summary>Whether the loose takeover group — the Dashboard, Notifications and Tools — is
    /// offered at all: with both features off it would be an empty group. Sessions
    /// and Pull requests have a group of their own. The name is older than that and
    /// is kept; nothing persists it.</summary>
    private bool SurfaceSwitcherVisible => ToolsOffered || DashboardOffered || NotificationsOffered;

    /// <summary>Whether the view switch offers the Roadmap: the catalog offers its
    /// surface, which it does while its feature is on. The option decides what is on
    /// screen.</summary>
    private bool RoadmapViewOffered => ViewOffered(RoadmapViewId);

    /// <summary>Whether the roadmap is on screen: the Roadmap view, in the
    /// workspace, with its feature on.</summary>
    private bool RoadmapViewVisible => WorkspaceVisible && EffectiveView == RoadmapViewId;

    /// <summary>Whether a view's option reads pressed: it is the view on screen.
    /// During a takeover no view is on screen, so none reads pressed.</summary>
    private bool ViewOptionPressed(string viewId) => WorkspaceVisible && EffectiveView == viewId;

    /// <summary>
    /// A press on a view's option: that view becomes the workspace's main view, and
    /// a takeover that was up closes, so the press is also the way back.
    /// <para>
    /// The option already pressed changes nothing — one view is always showing, so
    /// there is nothing to switch off to. The Devbook and the Inbox are takeovers,
    /// so a view option is the way back from them as from the others.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private void ShowView(string viewId) => ShowView(viewId, NoQuery);

    /// <summary>A view asked for with a query — a jump's plan or entry, which the view
    /// follows once (local ADR 0040 §3, §6).</summary>
    private void ShowView(string viewId, IReadOnlyDictionary<string, string> query)
    {
        if (!WorkspaceVisible) CloseSurface();

        SetView(viewId, query);
    }

    /// <summary>Makes <paramref name="viewId"/> the main view and remembers it for the
    /// next fresh shell instance by its surface id. Ask AI follows it by itself:
    /// it answers from whichever area is on screen.</summary>
    private void SetView(string viewId, IReadOnlyDictionary<string, string> query)
    {
        _view = viewId;
        _viewQuery = query;
        ShellNavigation.SetLastView(viewId);
        KeepRepositoryScopeSingleWhereOnlyOneFits();
    }

    /// <summary>Opens or closes the panel. Closing it ends an ask in flight: the
    /// answer would land in a panel nobody is looking at, and the quota it costs
    /// is better kept for a question the reader waits for.</summary>
    private void ToggleAiPanel()
    {
        _aiExpanded = !_aiExpanded;
        if (!_aiExpanded) CancelAsk();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Say whether this window offers the Sessions pane — the shell's half of
    /// <c>open_dashboard</c>, registered with <see cref="SessionsSurfaceActivator"/>
    /// while this window is alive.
    /// <para>
    /// It answers and never navigates. Every agent run calls <c>open_dashboard</c> when
    /// it starts, so a window that moved to the Sessions pane here would move whenever
    /// an agent did something, pulling the reader off whatever they were in the middle
    /// of. Navigating is the reader's act alone: the takeover groups in the header.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Inside <c>InvokeAsync</c> because the request arrives on a request thread with no
    /// relationship to this renderer, and the feature is read there so the answer
    /// describes the window as it is at the moment it is asked.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task<DeliverySurfaceActivation> DescribeSessionsAsync(CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        var answer = DeliverySurfaceActivation.Unattached;

        await InvokeAsync(() =>
        {
            cancellationToken.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();

            // Switched off is a real answer, not a failure: the area's key gates the
            // tab, so there is no pane for the reader to open.
            answer = SessionsOffered
                ? DeliverySurfaceActivation.Available
                : DeliverySurfaceActivation.Disabled;
        });

        return answer;
    }

    private void ToggleTools() => ToggleSurface(ToolsSurfaceId);

    private void ToggleDashboard() => ToggleSurface(DashboardSurfaceId);

    /// <summary>The Notifications option: opens the centre on what is waiting, or returns
    /// to the workspace when it is already on screen.</summary>
    private void ToggleNotifications() => ToggleSurface(NotificationsSurfaceId);

    /// <summary>
    /// The centre asked for from outside the shell — a Windows notification pressed — open on
    /// the notification named. Never closes it: a second press on the same notification shows
    /// the centre again rather than toggling it away. A centre open in a window of its own
    /// answers the same request there (NotificationCentreRequests keeps every listener).
    /// </summary>
    private void OpenNotificationCentre(Guid? notificationId) =>
        _ = InvokeAsync(() =>
        {
            if (!NotificationsOffered) return;

            OpenTakeover(
                NotificationsSurfaceId,
                notificationId is { } id
                    ? new Dictionary<string, string>(StringComparer.Ordinal) { [NotificationsSurfaceRegistration.NotificationKey] = id.ToString() }
                    : NoQuery);
            StateHasChanged();
        });

    private void ToggleSessions() => ToggleSurface(SessionsSurfaceId);

    private void TogglePullRequests() => ToggleSurface(PullRequestsSurfaceId);

    /// <summary>The Inbox option: opens the Inbox in place of the main view, or
    /// returns to that view when the Inbox is already on screen.</summary>
    private void ToggleInbox() => ToggleSurface(InboxSurfaceId);

    /// <summary>What the Inbox option's press will do.</summary>
    private string InboxOptionTitle => InboxVisible ? $"Back to {EffectiveViewTitle}" : "Open the Inbox";

    /// <summary>
    /// The Devbook on screen for something opened from outside it — an entry's
    /// reference, a Brain note's badge — unless it already is. Opened over the
    /// view, which waits underneath, so the Devbook option or Escape goes back to
    /// the list the reader pressed it from.
    /// <para>
    /// Never onto a Devbook the shell does not offer — its flag off, or nothing for
    /// it to show: the surface would not render, yet it would be remembered, and the
    /// flag coming back would put the reader in the Devbook unasked. The callers say
    /// why out loud before they get here; this is the backstop.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private void ShowDevbook()
    {
        if (!DevbookPaneOptionVisible || DevbookVisible) return;

        ToggleSurface(DevbookSurfaceId);
    }

    /// <summary>Opens a takeover, or returns to the workspace if it was already the
    /// one on screen. Assigning rather than flipping a flag is what makes the
    /// takeovers exclusive: opening one is the same act as closing the other.</summary>
    private void ToggleSurface(string surfaceId)
    {
        if (TakeoverVisible(surfaceId)) CloseSurface();
        else OpenTakeover(surfaceId, NoQuery);
    }

    /// <summary>Puts a takeover on screen with the query it was asked with — opened,
    /// never toggled, so a jump to the takeover already on screen keeps it there and
    /// follows the new query.</summary>
    private void OpenTakeover(string surfaceId, IReadOnlyDictionary<string, string> query)
    {
        // Focus moves onto a takeover as it opens, never onto one already on screen:
        // a jump that only hands it a new query must not pull focus out of it.
        _focusSurfacePending = !TakeoverVisible(surfaceId);
        _takeover = surfaceId;
        _takeoverQuery = query;

        // A takeover unmounts the main view, and the view it remounts on closing is a
        // new one: the jump's plan or entry it was asked with was followed already,
        // so it is not handed again.
        _viewQuery = NoQuery;
        RememberSurface();
        KeepRepositoryScopeSingleWhereOnlyOneFits();
    }

    /// <summary>Back to the workspace. The view was never touched, so there is
    /// nothing to restore — it is simply visible again.</summary>
    private void CloseSurface()
    {
        _takeover = null;
        _takeoverQuery = NoQuery;
        RememberSurface();
        KeepRepositoryScopeSingleWhereOnlyOneFits();
    }

    /// <summary>What the reader is looking at, written for the next fresh shell
    /// instance by its surface id; nothing for the workspace.</summary>
    private void RememberSurface() => ShellNavigation.SetLastSurface(_takeover);

    /// <summary>
    /// An AI session a Backlog entry names, opened from the entry: the sessions list
    /// on screen with that session's row marked and focused. A list switched off is
    /// said out loud rather than forced open over a setting the reader chose.
    /// </summary>
    private Task OpenSessionAsync(string sessionId)
    {
        if (!SessionsOffered)
        {
            Toasts.Publish(ToastMessage.Warning(
                "The sessions list is switched off, so this session cannot be opened here.",
                SessionsOffTestId));

            return Task.CompletedTask;
        }

        OpenTakeover(SessionsSurfaceId, new Dictionary<string, string>(StringComparer.Ordinal) { [SessionQueryKey] = sessionId });

        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    private const string SessionsOffTestId = Backlog.Desktop.UI.PullRequests.PullRequestsSurface.SessionsOffTestId;

    /// <summary>Whether the host composed the owner's notification centre at all.</summary>
    private bool _notificationCentreComposed;

    /// <summary>The listener on the requests to open the centre from outside the shell.</summary>
    private IDisposable? _notificationRequests;

    /// <summary>
    /// A pull request a card in a task's side panel names,
    /// opened from the card: the pull requests list on screen with its row marked.
    /// The mirror of <see cref="OpenSessionAsync"/>, on the same terms.
    /// </summary>
    private Task OpenPullRequestAsync(string key)
    {
        if (!PullRequestsOffered)
        {
            Toasts.Publish(ToastMessage.Warning(
                "The pull requests list is switched off, so this pull request cannot be opened here.",
                PullRequestsOffTestId));

            return Task.CompletedTask;
        }

        OpenTakeover(
            PullRequestsSurfaceId,
            new Dictionary<string, string>(StringComparer.Ordinal) { [Backlog.Desktop.UI.PullRequests.PullRequestsSurface.PullRequestKey] = key });

        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    private const string PullRequestsOffTestId = "in-progress-pull-requests-off";

    /// <summary>The sessions and open pull requests the Goals view and a task's
    /// side panel draw from, read by either and kept for both. Rebuilt with
    /// the page, which the Router remounts on every route change — so a trip to
    /// Settings and back reads again, which is what that trip is usually for.</summary>
    private WorkInProgressReader _work = new(null, null);

    /// <summary>The Tasks detail panel's "Sessions and pull requests", for the
    /// selected entry. Offered while the Sessions or the Pull requests feature is on:
    /// the section draws only the kinds that are. Built once, and again on every
    /// feature change, rather than as a new fragment on every render — see
    /// <see cref="BuildLinkedWork"/>.</summary>
    private RenderFragment<EntryRow>? LinkedWorkFor => _linkedWork;

    private RenderFragment<EntryRow>? _linkedWork;

    /// <summary>The window's <see cref="TasksViewWiring"/>, or null in a host that
    /// composes none.</summary>
    private TasksViewWiring? _tasksViewWiring;

    /// <summary>Hands the Tasks views the Brain badges, the scenario screenshots and
    /// a task's sessions and pull requests — what the shell used to pass the Tasks
    /// pane itself — again whenever a feature switch rebuilds the last.</summary>
    private void WireTasksViews()
    {
        if (_tasksViewWiring is not { } wiring) return;

        wiring.BrainNotesFor = BrainNotesForTask;
        wiring.DevbookImagesFor = DevbookImagesForEntry;
        wiring.LinkedWork = _linkedWork;
    }

    /// <summary>The window's Dashboard wiring, or null in a host that composes none.</summary>
    private Backlog.Modules.Dashboard.UI.DashboardSurfaceWiring? _dashboardWiring;

    /// <summary>What the shell hands the Dashboard's parts: their Ask AI, while the
    /// assistant is on and a Dashboard source answers — again whenever a feature switch
    /// moves that. Raised on this page's dispatcher, which owns the panel it opens.</summary>
    private Func<string, Task>? _dashboardAskAi;

    private void WireDashboard()
    {
        if (_dashboardWiring is not { } wiring) return;

        _dashboardAskAi = DashboardAskAiOffered
            ? title => InvokeAsync(() => AskAboutDashboardPartAsync(title))
            : null;
        wiring.AskAi = _dashboardAskAi;
    }

    /// <summary>The fragment behind <see cref="LinkedWorkFor"/>. It reads the flags
    /// and the reader as it draws, so only whether it is offered at all is decided
    /// here, and that is a feature's to change.</summary>
    private RenderFragment<EntryRow>? BuildLinkedWork()
    {
        if (!SessionsOffered && !PullRequestsOffered) return null;

        return row => builder => builder.AddContent(0, LinkedWorkSectionFor(row));
    }

    private RenderFragment LinkedWorkSectionFor(EntryRow row) =>
        @<LinkedWorkSection @key="row.Key"
                            Row="row"
                            Reader="_work"
                            OnOpenSession="OpenSessionAsync"
                            OnOpenPullRequest="OpenPullRequestAsync"
                            ShowSessions="SessionsOffered"
                            ShowPullRequests="PullRequestsOffered" />;

    /// <summary>
    /// The repository an entry's reference asked the Devbook pane to read, and the
    /// scope anchor it was asked under — or null when nothing has asked, or the
    /// entry's repository was the anchor anyway.
    /// <para>
    /// Held beside the scope rather than written into it, and that is the decision.
    /// Scoping the list to the entry's repository would also show the pane the right
    /// devbook, and it would narrow the backlog the reader was working in to answer a
    /// question about one chapter — and with nothing scoped there is no anchor at
    /// all, so the pane would have nothing to read. Keyed on the anchor so it lasts
    /// exactly as long as the scope the reader left alone: choosing a repository in
    /// the header is choosing what the pane reads, and the request is forgotten.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The anchor is <see cref="DevbookRequestAnchor"/>, never
    /// <see cref="TasksDesktopState.AnchorRepositoryAlias"/>: that one is the empty
    /// string both with nothing pressed and with the Brain as anchor, so a request
    /// asked under one outlived a press to the other and the Brain chip could not
    /// bring the Brain back. And forgotten means dropped
    /// (<see cref="ForgetDevbookRepositoryRequestLeftBehind"/>), not set aside: a
    /// request merely ignored while the anchor differed came back with it.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private DevbookRepositoryRequest? _devbookRepositoryRequest;

    private sealed record DevbookRepositoryRequest(string? Anchor, string RepositoryAlias);

    /// <summary>The scope's anchor as a request is keyed on it: the first member,
    /// <see cref="BrainScope.Value"/> for the Brain, or null when nothing is pressed —
    /// the two states <see cref="TasksDesktopState.AnchorRepositoryAlias"/> spells
    /// alike.</summary>
    private string? DevbookRequestAnchor =>
        State.SelectedRepositoryAliases is [var first, ..] ? first : null;

    /// <summary>Drops the pane's repository request once the scope's anchor has moved
    /// off the one it was asked under, so returning to that anchor later finds the
    /// pane on the anchor rather than on a chapter followed long before.</summary>
    private void ForgetDevbookRepositoryRequestLeftBehind()
    {
        if (_devbookRepositoryRequest is { } asked
            && !string.Equals(asked.Anchor, DevbookRequestAnchor, StringComparison.Ordinal))
        {
            _devbookRepositoryRequest = null;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>The last reference pressed for the Devbook, handed to the surface as
    /// its <c>chapter</c> and <c>request</c> query keys. The token makes the same
    /// reference pressed twice two requests; the surface remembers, per window,
    /// which token it already followed, so a Devbook hidden and shown again does not
    /// pull the reader back to the task's chapter.</summary>
    private DevbookChapterRequest? _devbookOpenRequest;

    private sealed record DevbookChapterRequest(string Reference, string Token);

    private static DevbookChapterRequest DevbookChapter(string reference) =>
        new(reference, Guid.NewGuid().ToString("N"));

    /// <summary>The repository the Devbook pane reads: the one an entry's reference
    /// asked for while the scope it was asked under still stands, else the scope's
    /// anchor.</summary>
    private string DevbookRepositoryAlias =>
        _devbookRepositoryRequest is { } asked
        && string.Equals(asked.Anchor, DevbookRequestAnchor, StringComparison.Ordinal)
            ? asked.RepositoryAlias
            : _devbookSwitch is { } chosen
              && string.Equals(chosen.Scope, DevbookSwitchScope, StringComparison.Ordinal)
              && DevbookSwitchRepositoryAlias is { } partner
                ? chosen.ShowsBrain ? BrainScope.Value : partner
                : State.AnchorRepositoryAlias;

    /// <summary>
    /// Which side of the Devbook pane's <c>Brain | repo</c> switch the reader
    /// pressed, and the scope it was pressed under (local ADR 0027 §4).
    /// <para>
    /// Shell state beside <see cref="_devbookRepositoryRequest"/>, for the same
    /// reason: it chooses what the pane reads and must not touch what the header
    /// scopes, so it never goes near the scope or the task filter. Keyed on the
    /// whole scope, so any chip press drops it and the pane reopens on the anchor;
    /// and not remembered across a restart, which keeps the scope alone (ADR 0027
    /// §5).
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private DevbookSwitchChoice? _devbookSwitch;

    private sealed record DevbookSwitchChoice(string Scope, bool ShowsBrain);

    /// <summary>The scope as one string, in its order, to key the switch on.</summary>
    private string DevbookSwitchScope => string.Join("|", State.SelectedRepositoryAliases);

    /// <summary>
    /// The repository on the other side of the switch: drawn only while the Brain
    /// and at least one repository are in the scope, in a host that composes a
    /// Brain. The anchor when a repository is the anchor; with the Brain as anchor,
    /// the first repository taken after it — both are the first repository in the
    /// scope's order.
    /// </summary>
    private string? DevbookSwitchRepositoryAlias =>
        _brainFolders is not null
        && IsFeatureEnabled(DevbookFeatures.RepositoryDevbook)
        && State.BrainInScope
        && BrainScope.Repositories(State.SelectedRepositoryAliases) is [var first, ..]
            ? first
            : null;

    /// <summary>A press on the pane's switch. It changes what the pane reads and
    /// nothing else; an entry's reference the pane was following gives way to it,
    /// because the reader just chose.</summary>
    private void OnDevbookShowsBrainChanged(bool showsBrain)
    {
        if (DevbookSwitchRepositoryAlias is null) return;

        _devbookSwitch = new DevbookSwitchChoice(DevbookSwitchScope, showsBrain);
        _devbookRepositoryRequest = null;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// One of an entry's Devbook references, opened from the entry: the Devbook
    /// on screen, reading the entry's repository, on that chapter.
    /// <para>
    /// The mirror of <see cref="OpenSessionAsync"/>: the entry belongs to Tasks and
    /// the chapter to Devbook, and the shell is the one place holding both. Opened
    /// over the view, which waits underneath — the Devbook option or Escape goes
    /// back to the list the reader pressed it from.
    /// A repository with no devbook to show, or the Devbook switched off, is said out
    /// loud rather than opening an empty Devbook.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private Task OpenDevbookReferenceAsync(DevbookReferenceRequest request)
    {
        var alias = string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(request.RepositoryAlias)
            ? State.AnchorRepositoryAlias
            : request.RepositoryAlias;

        if (!IsFeatureEnabled(DevbookFeatures.RepositoryDevbook)
            || alias.Length == 0
            || Devbook.VisibleAreas(alias).Count == 0)
        {
            Toasts.Publish(ToastMessage.Warning(
                alias.Length == 0
                    ? "This entry names no repository, so there is no Devbook to open it in."
                    : $"The Devbook of {alias} is not available here, so this reference cannot be opened.",
                DevbookUnavailableTestId));

            return Task.CompletedTask;
        }

        _devbookRepositoryRequest = string.Equals(alias, State.AnchorRepositoryAlias, StringComparison.Ordinal)
            ? null
            : new DevbookRepositoryRequest(DevbookRequestAnchor, alias);
        _devbookOpenRequest = DevbookChapter(request.Reference);

        // The option may only now be on offer: with nothing scoped there was no
        // repository to read until the request named one.
        ShowDevbook();

        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    private const string DevbookUnavailableTestId = "tasks-devbook-unavailable";

    /// <summary>
    /// The Devbook asked for through this window's <see cref="ISurfaceNavigator"/>
    /// (local ADR 0040 §3) — by the Devbook itself, for a jump out of what it shows.
    /// <list type="bullet">
    /// <item>A <c>chapter</c> in a repository — a Brain note's <c>repo:</c> link
    /// (local ADR 0027 §6) or another repository's search result — takes the road a
    /// task's reference takes (<see cref="OpenRepositoryChapterAsync"/>), so the
    /// header scope stays as the reader left it and the pane's switch, or the scope,
    /// brings the Brain back.</item>
    /// <item>A <c>chapter</c> in the Brain — a Brain search result — is
    /// <see cref="OpenBrainChapter"/>.</item>
    /// <item>No chapter, and the Brain or the switch's repository as <c>scope</c>: a
    /// side of the pane's <c>Brain | repo</c> switch picked.</item>
    /// </list>
    /// </summary>
    private Task ShowDevbookAsync(SurfaceRequest request)
    {
        var scope = request.Value(DevbookSurfaceQuery.Scope) is { } given ? DevbookSurfaceQuery.AliasFor(given) : null;

        if (request.Value(DevbookSurfaceQuery.Chapter) is not { Length: > 0 } chapter)
        {
            if (scope is not null
                && DevbookSwitchRepositoryAlias is { } partner
                && (BrainScope.Is(scope) || string.Equals(scope, partner, StringComparison.Ordinal)))
            {
                OnDevbookShowsBrainChanged(BrainScope.Is(scope));
            }

            ShowDevbook();
            return Task.CompletedTask;
        }

        // Without a scope, the window's own: the anchor, which may be the Brain.
        var target = scope ?? State.AnchorRepositoryAlias;
        if (BrainScope.Is(target)) return OpenBrainNoteAsync(chapter);

        return OpenRepositoryChapterAsync(target, chapter);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// A Brain page at <paramref name="reference"/>, in the pane. The Brain is no
    /// repository, so it is asked of the pane directly: it changes what the pane
    /// reads and never the header's scope or the task filter, holds only while the
    /// scope it was asked under stands, and the switch the reader may have pressed
    /// gives way to it.
    /// </summary>
    private void OpenBrainChapter(string reference)
    {
        _devbookSwitch = null;
        _devbookRepositoryRequest = BrainScope.Is(State.AnchorRepositoryAlias)
            ? null
            : new DevbookRepositoryRequest(DevbookRequestAnchor, BrainScope.Value);
        _devbookOpenRequest = DevbookChapter(reference);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// One repository's devbook in the pane, at a chapter, for a reader who chose it
    /// from outside the pane's own menu — a Brain note's link or a search result.
    /// </summary>
    private Task OpenRepositoryChapterAsync(string repositoryAlias, string reference)
    {
        // The pane was on the Brain, perhaps through the switch: the reader just
        // chose a repository, so the switch's choice gives way — else a chapter of
        // the anchor repository would leave the pane on the Brain.
        _devbookSwitch = null;

        // The alias as configured, not as it was spelled: a link resolves
        // case-insensitively, and the shell compares aliases exactly.
        var alias = Devbook.Repository(repositoryAlias)?.Alias ?? repositoryAlias;
        return OpenDevbookReferenceAsync(new DevbookReferenceRequest(alias, reference));
    }

    private void OnSurfaceKeyDown(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        if (e.Key == "Escape") CloseSurface();
    }

    /// <summary>The same Escape, heard at the document because focus is on
    /// <c>&lt;body&gt;</c> — where a focused control that went disabled, such as a
    /// Refresh button while its read runs, leaves it — and so never reached the
    /// surface's own handler. The script checks a closing surface is open; this
    /// checks again, because the render may have moved on since the key.</summary>
    [JSInvokable]
    public Task CloseSurfaceOnEscapeAsync()
    {
        // The Roadmap view is the workspace and has no Escape of its own here, and
        // a takeover registered to keep Escape — the Inbox — keeps it for its own keys.
        if (TakeoverOnScreen is not { EscapeCloses: true }) return Task.CompletedTask;

        CloseSurface();
        StateHasChanged();
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    /// <summary>The question box's text, kept so the panel reopens with it. Not a
    /// render: see <see cref="AskAiQuestion"/>.</summary>
    private void KeepAiQuestion(string question) => _aiQuestion = question;

    /// <summary>Whether the Dashboard's parts offer Ask AI: the assistant is on and a
    /// Dashboard source was composed, so the panel the control opens can answer.</summary>
    private bool DashboardAskAiOffered =>
        IsFeatureEnabled(AppFeatures.AiAssistant) && _aiSources.ContainsKey("dashboard");

    /// <summary>
    /// A dashboard part's Ask AI: the panel opens on the Dashboard with a question
    /// that names the part, for the reader to finish.
    /// <para>
    /// The chart goes into the question and not into the content. The Dashboard's
    /// source sends every tab's figures whichever part was pressed, because what is on
    /// screen is not a source's business; the reader's question is where something
    /// about the screen belongs, and the prefix is just the start of one. A question
    /// already begun about the same part is kept rather than reset, so a second press
    /// does not throw away what was typed. The answer to an earlier question goes:
    /// it was about something else — and so does an ask still in flight, ended the
    /// way closing the panel ends one, or it would land under the new question and
    /// read as the answer to it.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task AskAboutDashboardPartAsync(string title)
    {
        var prefix = $"About the \"{title}\" chart: ";

        CancelAsk();
        _aiExpanded = true;
        if (!_aiQuestion.StartsWith(prefix, StringComparison.Ordinal)) _aiQuestion = prefix;
        _aiAnswer = null;
        StateHasChanged();

        try
        {
            // The box is drawn by the render just requested, and backlogFocus waits a
            // few frames for it, so the reader can type straight after the prefix.
            await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("backlogFocus", AskAiQuestion.InputId, false);
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // Focus is an improvement on where the browser would have left it, not
            // something the panel depends on — the same rule the surfaces' focus keeps.
        }
    }

    /// <summary>An ask, from the box's button or its Ctrl+Enter. The question is
    /// the one the box already kept here; the text it was asked with is taken
    /// again so the two cannot differ.</summary>
    private async Task AskAiAsync(string question)
    {
        _aiQuestion = question;
        if (EffectiveAiScope is not { } scope || !_aiSources.TryGetValue(scope, out var source)) return;

        // One token per ask, and a new ask ends the one before it: an answer to
        // the question the reader has moved on from is not worth its quota, and a
        // compose still reading a store for it is not worth its I/O. The button is
        // disabled while an ask is in flight, so this reaches a second ask only by
        // the keyboard shortcut — and the one in flight is the older of the two.
        var ask = ReplaceAsk();
        _aiBusy = true;
        _aiAnswer = null;

        try
        {
            // The scoped area composes its own content for the question, within
            // the budget the deployment can take in one turn. The shell hands over
            // the body and learns nothing about what is in it.
            //
            // Its own try, because the source reads a store — the tool catalog's
            // JSON, the plan's SQLite file, the devbook database, GitHub — and any
            // of those can throw. That throw is a fact about the area, not about
            // the assistant, so it is said as one; and it must not reach the error
            // boundary, which would take the page for a question that was never
            // sent.
            AiContent content;
            try
            {
                content = await source.ComposeAsync(new AiContentRequest(_aiQuestion, AiContentBudget.DefaultCharacters), ask.Token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (ask.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                return;
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                Toasts.Publish(ToastMessage.Error($"Could not gather the {source.AreaTitle} content: {ex.Message}", AiErrorTestId));
                return;
            }

            var response = await AzureFoundryChat.AskAsync(new AzureFoundryChatRequest(content.Body, _aiQuestion), ask.Token);
            if (!ask.IsCancellationRequested) _aiAnswer = response.Answer;
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (ask.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // The reader closed the panel, left the page, or asked again: nothing
            // to say about an answer nobody is waiting for.
        }
        catch (AzureFoundryException ex) when (!ask.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // The one exception the client throws for every way an answer fails
            // to be one: a bad status, an endpoint that could not be reached, and
            // a request that ran out of time - the pipeline's timeout included,
            // which used to arrive here as Polly's own exception, past the two
            // transport catches this handler kept, and stop the whole page.
            Toasts.Publish(ToastMessage.Error(ex.Message, AiErrorTestId));
        }
        catch (AzureFoundryException)
        {
            // Cancelled mid-flight and the client said so in its own words; the
            // reader has already moved on.
        }
        finally
        {
            // Only the ask that is still current clears the flag: a cancelled one
            // finishing late must not mark its successor idle.
            if (ReferenceEquals(_aiAsk, ask)) _aiBusy = false;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>The in-flight ask's cancellation, or null when none is in flight.
    /// Cancelled by the next ask, by closing the panel, and on dispose.</summary>
    private CancellationTokenSource? _aiAsk;

    /// <summary>Ends whatever ask is in flight and starts the token for the next.</summary>
    private CancellationTokenSource ReplaceAsk()
    {
        CancelAsk();
        return _aiAsk = new CancellationTokenSource();
    }

    /// <summary>Ends the ask in flight, if any. Disposed here rather than at the
    /// end of the ask, so a token a late continuation still reads is a cancelled
    /// token and not a disposed one.</summary>
    private void CancelAsk()
    {
        var ask = _aiAsk;
        if (ask is null) return;

        _aiAsk = null;
        _aiBusy = false;
        ask.Cancel();
        ask.Dispose();
    }

    private bool InboxPaneOptionVisible => TakeoverOffered(InboxSurfaceId);

    /// <summary>The Inbox pane's own id, the one its keyboard shortcuts are
    /// registered under; see <c>InboxPane</c>.</summary>
    private const string InboxPaneId = "inbox-pane";

    /// <summary>Whether the Inbox has the screen. The same shape as the takeovers
    /// above: its flag switched off returns the reader to the workspace.</summary>
    private bool InboxVisible => TakeoverVisible(InboxSurfaceId);

    /// <summary>Whether there is any knowledge to offer: the Devbook's surface in the
    /// catalog — registered, its flag on — and something for it to read. The shell
    /// asks Devbook rather than working it out from folder settings itself.</summary>
    private bool DevbookPaneOptionVisible =>
        Catalog.TryGet(DevbookSurfaceId, out _)
        && (DevbookBrainOffered || Devbook.VisibleAreas(DevbookRepositoryAlias).Count > 0);

    /// <summary>The Devbook's id in the surface catalog.</summary>
    private const string DevbookSurfaceId = DevbookSurfaceQuery.SurfaceId;

    /// <summary>The registered takeovers the header draws after the view switch, in the
    /// catalog's order: every one this window offers that the header does not place
    /// itself — today the Devbook.</summary>
    private IEnumerable<ShellSurface> RegisteredTakeoversOffered =>
        Catalog.Surfaces.Where(surface =>
            surface.Group == ShellSurfaceGroup.Takeover
            && !PlacedTakeovers.Contains(surface.Id)
            && TakeoverOffered(surface.Id));

    /// <summary>The registered takeover on screen among those, or null.</summary>
    private ShellSurface? RegisteredSurfaceOnScreen =>
        TakeoverOnScreen is { } shown && !PlacedTakeovers.Contains(shown.Id) ? shown : null;

    /// <summary>A registered takeover's header option: opens it in place of the main
    /// view, or returns to that view when it is already on screen.</summary>
    private void ToggleRegisteredSurface(string surfaceId) => ToggleSurface(surfaceId);

    /// <summary>The host's windows, or null where it opens none and the header offers
    /// no ⧉ (local ADR 0040 §4).</summary>
    private IShellWindows? _shellWindows;

    /// <summary>The ⧉ button's name: what its press opens, named as the option is.</summary>
    private string PopOutName(ShellSurface surface) => $"Open {SurfaceOptionName(surface)} in a new window";

    /// <summary>
    /// The ⧉ beside a header item's option (local ADR 0040 §4), written once for
    /// every item the header draws: nothing where the host opens no windows, or
    /// where the catalog holds no such surface to open one on.
    /// </summary>
    private RenderFragment PopOutButton(string surfaceId) =>
        _shellWindows is not null && Catalog.TryGet(surfaceId, out var surface)
            ? PopOutButton(surfaceId, PopOutName(surface))
            : NoPopOut;

    private RenderFragment PopOutButton(string surfaceId, string name) =>
        @<IconButton BaseClass="header-surface-item__pop"
                     AriaLabel="@name"
                     Title="@name"
                     TestId="@($"{surfaceId}-pop-out")"
                     OnClick="() => PopOut(surfaceId)">⧉</IconButton>;

    private static readonly RenderFragment NoPopOut = _ => { };

    /// <summary>
    /// ⧉ pressed: one more window on the surface (local ADR 0040 §4). It opens on what
    /// this window would show of it — the Devbook on the repository or the Brain it
    /// reads here. Only its scope: the chapter last pressed and the switch's partner
    /// were this window's, and the new window has a scope of its own.
    /// </summary>
    private void PopOut(string surfaceId)
    {
        if (_shellWindows is null || !Catalog.TryGet(surfaceId, out var surface)) return;

        var query = SurfaceQuery(surface)
            .Where(pair => string.Equals(pair.Key, DevbookSurfaceQuery.Scope, StringComparison.Ordinal))
            .ToDictionary(pair => pair.Key, pair => pair.Value, StringComparer.Ordinal);
        _shellWindows.Open(surfaceId, query);
    }

    /// <summary>A registered surface's word in the header — the Devbook's names what
    /// it shows, the Brain or the Devbook.</summary>
    private string SurfaceOptionName(ShellSurface surface) =>
        surface.Id == DevbookSurfaceId ? DevbookPaneOptionName : surface.Title;

    /// <summary>What a registered takeover's option press will do.</summary>
    private string SurfaceOptionTitle(ShellSurface surface) =>
        RegisteredSurfaceOnScreen?.Id == surface.Id ? $"Back to {EffectiveViewTitle}" : $"Open the {SurfaceOptionName(surface)}";

    /// <summary>The Brain's accent on the Devbook's option while it reads the Brain.</summary>
    private string? SurfaceOptionCssClass(ShellSurface surface) =>
        surface.Id == DevbookSurfaceId && DevbookPaneShowsBrain ? "header-group__option--brain" : null;

    /// <summary>What a registered surface's component is handed: its query and the
    /// close callback (<see cref="ShellSurface"/>).</summary>
    private Dictionary<string, object?> SurfaceParameters(ShellSurface surface) => new(StringComparer.Ordinal)
    {
        [ShellSurface.QueryParameter] = SurfaceQuery(surface),
        [ShellSurface.CloseParameter] = EventCallback.Factory.Create(this, CloseSurface),
    };

    /// <summary>
    /// The query this window shows a registered surface with. The Devbook's says
    /// what this window's scope makes it read — the anchor and not the whole scope:
    /// the Devbook reads one repository's folders, and the first one taken is the one
    /// the reader was already reading, unless an entry's reference asked for another
    /// (see <see cref="DevbookRepositoryAlias"/>) — the partner of its switch, and the
    /// last chapter pressed for it.
    /// </summary>
    private IReadOnlyDictionary<string, string> SurfaceQuery(ShellSurface surface)
    {
        if (surface.Id != DevbookSurfaceId) return TakeoverOnScreen?.Id == surface.Id ? _takeoverQuery : NoQuery;

        var query = new Dictionary<string, string>(StringComparer.Ordinal);

        query[DevbookSurfaceQuery.Scope] = DevbookSurfaceQuery.ScopeFor(DevbookRepositoryAlias);
        if (DevbookSwitchRepositoryAlias is { } partner) query[DevbookSurfaceQuery.Switch] = partner;
        if (_devbookOpenRequest is { } request)
        {
            query[DevbookSurfaceQuery.Chapter] = request.Reference;
            query[DevbookSurfaceQuery.Request] = request.Token;
        }

        return query;
    }

    /// <summary>A surface asked for through this window's navigator (local ADR 0040
    /// §3): answered on this page's dispatcher, then drawn.</summary>
    private void HandleSurfaceRequested(SurfaceRequest request) => _ = InvokeAsync(() => ShowRequestedSurfaceAsync(request));

    /// <summary>A surface asked of this main window by a popped-out one — its Show in
    /// main window, or a jump it cannot show itself (<see cref="MainWindowSurfaces"/>).
    /// Answers whether this window shows it now.</summary>
    private async Task<bool> ShowFromAnotherWindowAsync(SurfaceRequest request)
    {
        var shown = false;
        await InvokeAsync(async () => shown = await ShowRequestedSurfaceAsync(request));
        return shown;
    }

    /// <summary>Shows the surface <paramref name="request"/> names, then draws;
    /// false for a surface this shell cannot show.</summary>
    private async Task<bool> ShowRequestedSurfaceAsync(SurfaceRequest request)
    {
        switch (request.SurfaceId)
        {
            case DevbookSurfaceId:
                await ShowDevbookAsync(request);
                break;
            case SessionsSurfaceId when request.Value(SessionQueryKey) is { Length: > 0 } session:
                // A task's session: the list on it, or said out loud when it is off.
                await OpenSessionAsync(session);
                break;
            case var takeoverId when Catalog.TryGet(takeoverId, out var takeover)
                    && takeover.Group == ShellSurfaceGroup.Takeover
                    && TakeoverOffered(takeoverId):
                // A takeover, with the keys it registered: the session list on a
                // session, the pull requests on one, the centre on a notification.
                OpenTakeover(takeoverId, RegisteredKeysOf(takeover, request.Query));
                break;
            case var viewId when Catalog.TryGet(viewId, out var view) && view.Group == ShellSurfaceGroup.View:
                // A view, with the keys it registered: it follows a plan or an entry
                // itself, and says so out loud for an entry this backlog does not hold.
                ShowView(viewId, RegisteredKeysOf(view, request.Query));
                break;
            default:
                return false;
        }

        StateHasChanged();
        return true;
    }

    /// <summary>The key a task's session is asked under — a jump out of the Tasks
    /// views and a task's side panel.</summary>
    private const string SessionQueryKey = Backlog.Modules.Sessions.UI.SessionsSurfaceRegistration.SessionKey;

    /// <summary>Only the keys of <paramref name="query"/> the surface registered, in a
    /// new instance — the request it stands for.</summary>
    private static IReadOnlyDictionary<string, string> RegisteredKeysOf(ShellSurface surface, IReadOnlyDictionary<string, string> query)
    {
        var keys = surface.QueryKeys ?? [];
        return query
            .Where(pair => keys.Contains(pair.Key, StringComparer.Ordinal))
            .ToDictionary(pair => pair.Key, pair => pair.Value, StringComparer.Ordinal);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the pane has the Brain to offer (local ADR 0027 §4): it reads the
    /// Brain whenever nothing is pressed or the Brain is the anchor, and offers it
    /// beside a repository through the switch. Needs no folder — the Brain is always
    /// in the scope, and a pane on a Brain with no folder is where the person is
    /// asked for one. A host that composes no Brain offers none.
    /// </summary>
    private bool DevbookBrainOffered =>
        _brainFolders is not null
        && (BrainScope.Is(DevbookRepositoryAlias) || DevbookSwitchRepositoryAlias is not null);

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the Devbook pane shows the Brain rather than a repository — the
    /// shell's side of <c>DevbookPane.BrainMode</c>, so the header option can say so
    /// before the pane is open. <see cref="DevbookRepositoryAlias"/> already folds in
    /// nothing pressed, a Brain anchor, an entry's reference and the pane's
    /// <c>Brain | repo</c> switch, so this asks only whether that alias is the Brain
    /// in a host that composes one.
    /// </summary>
    private bool DevbookPaneShowsBrain => _brainFolders is not null && BrainScope.Is(DevbookRepositoryAlias);

    /// <summary>The Devbook option's word, and the name its title acts on.</summary>
    private string DevbookPaneOptionName => DevbookPaneShowsBrain ? BrainScope.Name : "Devbook";

    /// <summary>Whether the Devbook has the screen. The same shape as the takeovers
    /// above: with its flag switched off, or nothing left for it to show, the reader
    /// is back on the workspace.</summary>
    private bool DevbookVisible => TakeoverVisible(DevbookSurfaceId);

    private bool IsFeatureEnabled(string featureKey) => Features.IsEnabled(featureKey);

    /// <summary>The maturity flag an entry point wears — empty unless the feature
    /// behind it is both switched on and unfinished.
    ///
    /// <para>The settings screen shows a status whether or not the switch is on,
    /// because it is describing the switch. Here the badge is describing
    /// something you can reach and click, so a feature that is off has nothing to
    /// flag. Both halves of that live in <see cref="AppFeatureStatusBadge"/>
    /// rather than being spelled out at each of the entry points below.</para></summary>
    private string FeatureStatusSlug(string featureKey) =>
        AppFeatureStatusBadge.SlugFor(AppFeatures.All, Features, featureKey);

    private string? FeatureStatusTitle(string featureKey) =>
        AppFeatureStatusBadge.TitleFor(AppFeatures.All, Features, featureKey);

    /// <summary>
    /// A Tasks or Inbox change: the shell keeps its own state in step with it, and
    /// draws itself again only when the change moved something it draws.
    /// <para>
    /// The Tasks state announces every keystroke in an entry's body and every move
    /// of the selection, and the shell drew the whole page for each — the header, and
    /// every pane under it that takes a parameter Blazor cannot compare, the Tasks
    /// pane among them, which then drew a second time for its own subscription. The
    /// header draws none of what those change. So the side effects below run for
    /// every change, and the redraw only when <see cref="ShellInputs"/> differ from
    /// what the last render drew.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Compared after the side effects, because they can move an input themselves:
    /// narrowing the scope to its anchor raises another change, and a Devbook that
    /// lost what it shows leaves the screen. And always drawn while the session
    /// or pull request list is on screen: those read their tasks through this page's
    /// delegates and listen to nothing, so a row whose link or status moved in place
    /// reaches their cells only through this render.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private void HandleStateChanged() => _ = InvokeAsync(() =>
    {
        SynchronizeDevbookAvailability();
        ForgetDevbookRepositoryRequestLeftBehind();

        RememberRepositoryScope();

        if (SessionsVisible || PullRequestsVisible || !ReadShellInputs().Matches(_drawnShellInputs)) StateHasChanged();
    });

    /// <summary>What the last render drew of <see cref="ShellInputs"/>, read as it
    /// began; null before the first.</summary>
    private ShellInputs? _drawnShellInputs;

    private ShellInputs ReadShellInputs() => new(
        Scope: [.. State.SelectedRepositoryAliases],
        Repositories: [.. State.Repositories.Select(repository => (repository.Alias, repository.FullName))],
        Colours: [.. State.RepositoryColours.OrderBy(colour => colour.Key, StringComparer.Ordinal).Select(colour => (colour.Key, colour.Value))],
        ColoursVisible: State.RepositoryColoursVisible,
        ShowArchived: State.ShowArchivedRepositories,
        HasArchived: State.HasArchivedRepositories,
        GitHubSyncOffered: GitHubSyncOffered,
        GitHubSyncing: State.GitHubSyncing,
        DevbookOptionVisible: DevbookPaneOptionVisible,
        DevbookVisible: DevbookVisible,
        DevbookRepositoryAlias: DevbookRepositoryAlias,
        DevbookSwitchRepositoryAlias: DevbookSwitchRepositoryAlias);

    /// <summary>
    /// Everything the shell draws that a Tasks or Inbox change can move without one
    /// of the shell's own events firing: the scope chips and the Brain chip, the
    /// Colors switch and the identity marks, the GitHub check, the Devbook option and
    /// surface and what the Devbook reads — and through the scope, what the Dashboard and
    /// the two lists are handed. The rest of the page moves only by the shell's own
    /// handlers, which draw it anyway.
    /// <para>
    /// Copies, never the live lists: the scope the state holds is narrowed in place,
    /// and a snapshot that shared it would always match.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private sealed record ShellInputs(
        string[] Scope,
        (string Alias, string FullName)[] Repositories,
        (string Alias, int Colour)[] Colours,
        bool ColoursVisible,
        bool ShowArchived,
        bool HasArchived,
        bool GitHubSyncOffered,
        bool GitHubSyncing,
        bool DevbookOptionVisible,
        bool DevbookVisible,
        string DevbookRepositoryAlias,
        string? DevbookSwitchRepositoryAlias)
    {
        public bool Matches(ShellInputs? drawn) =>
            drawn is not null
            && Scope.SequenceEqual(drawn.Scope, StringComparer.Ordinal)
            && Repositories.SequenceEqual(drawn.Repositories)
            && Colours.SequenceEqual(drawn.Colours)
            && ColoursVisible == drawn.ColoursVisible
            && ShowArchived == drawn.ShowArchived
            && HasArchived == drawn.HasArchived
            && GitHubSyncOffered == drawn.GitHubSyncOffered
            && GitHubSyncing == drawn.GitHubSyncing
            && DevbookOptionVisible == drawn.DevbookOptionVisible
            && DevbookVisible == drawn.DevbookVisible
            && string.Equals(DevbookRepositoryAlias, drawn.DevbookRepositoryAlias, StringComparison.Ordinal)
            && string.Equals(DevbookSwitchRepositoryAlias, drawn.DevbookSwitchRepositoryAlias, StringComparison.Ordinal);
    }

    /// <summary>Whether the remembered repository scope is still to be handed to the
    /// state: true until a pass finds the repository registry readable.</summary>
    private bool _repositoryScopeRestorePending = true;

    /// <summary>
    /// Keeps the repository scope and <c>shell-navigation.json</c> in step. The first
    /// pass that finds the registry readable restores the remembered scope; every
    /// later one writes the scope as it now stands — pressed, narrowed or pruned by
    /// Settings — for the next fresh shell instance, and the store writes only on a
    /// change.
    /// <para>
    /// While the registry is unreadable it does neither. The state's scope is then
    /// built against a list nobody could read, and writing it would replace the
    /// reader's scope with that failure's: the next launch with a readable registry
    /// must still find the scope as it was left.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private void RememberRepositoryScope()
    {
        if (!State.RepositoryRegistryReadable) return;

        if (_repositoryScopeRestorePending)
        {
            _repositoryScopeRestorePending = false;
            State.RestoreRepositoryScope(ShellNavigation.RepositoryScope);
            return;
        }

        ShellNavigation.SetRepositoryScope(State.SelectedRepositoryAliases);
    }

    private void HandleFeaturesChanged() => _ = InvokeAsync(() =>
    {
        _linkedWork = BuildLinkedWork();
        WireTasksViews();
        WireDashboard();
        SynchronizeDevbookAvailability();

        // Switched on after the list loaded: check now rather than at a reload
        // that may not come. Only on the switch, so another flag moving does not
        // set off a check of its own.
        var gitHubOn = IsFeatureEnabled(TasksFeatures.GitHubIntegration);
        if (gitHubOn && !_gitHubIntegrationWasOn) CheckPullRequestsAfterLoad();
        _gitHubIntegrationWasOn = gitHubOn;

        StateHasChanged();
    });

    /// <summary>Whether the GitHub integration was on when the feature switches
    /// were last looked at. See <see cref="HandleFeaturesChanged"/>.</summary>
    private bool _gitHubIntegrationWasOn;

    /// <summary>The replication loop, or null in a host that composes none. The
    /// Inbox listens to it here rather than itself because the Inbox's UI may
    /// not see the sync adapter — the shell may, and forwarding one event is
    /// the whole of what it does with it.</summary>
    private TaskSyncWorker? _taskSync;

    /// <summary>This window's registration with <see cref="SessionsSurfaceActivator"/>,
    /// held only so it can be given up. Null where the host composed no activator.</summary>
    private IDisposable? _surfaceAttachment;

    /// <summary>This window's registration with <see cref="MainWindowSurfaces"/>, held
    /// so it can be given up. Null where the host composed none.</summary>
    private IDisposable? _mainWindowAttachment;

    private TaskSyncWorker? ResolveTaskSync()
    {
        try
        {
            return Services.GetService<TaskSyncWorker>();
        }
        catch (InvalidOperationException)
        {
            // Registered but not constructible here — the same case Settings
            // reads as "this host does not replicate".
            return null;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>A pull may have delivered a capture or another device's edits.
    /// Reloading either pane is a database read, so both are re-read only for a
    /// cycle that has finished and applied something it had not applied before —
    /// the Inbox, besides, only once it has loaded at all, since before that the
    /// first load will read whatever arrived. The worker raises this at both ends
    /// of a cycle and for cycles that changed nothing, and a list that started over
    /// on every one of those would replace its rows every five minutes for no
    /// reader's benefit.
    /// <para>
    /// The Inbox is re-read after a cycle that failed as well. A failure leaves the
    /// last summary as it was, so it cannot say whether pages merged before the
    /// failure — a pull that failed part-way has written them — and a capture that
    /// arrived in them is what the Inbox is for.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The Tasks reload is what the timestamp poll used to do — a pull writes
    /// the same database the poll watched — and the state keeps that poll's
    /// guard: a reload that arrives under a live caret is owed rather than
    /// dropped, and lands when the caret goes.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The task is not awaited — the event is synchronous and the renderer's
    /// dispatcher owns the continuation — so a failure inside it has nowhere
    /// to go but the toast. The catch is on this side of <c>InvokeAsync</c> so
    /// an unobserved exception cannot take the circuit down for a read the
    /// reader never asked for.
    /// </para></summary>
    private void HandleTaskSyncChanged() => _ = InvokeAsync(async () =>
    {
        if (_taskSync is not { IsSyncing: false } sync) return;

        // Read once: the worker writes it from its own thread.
        var summary = sync.LastSummary;
        var applied = summary is { Applied: > 0 } && summary.At != _lastAppliedSyncAt;
        var failed = sync.LastError is not null;

        if (applied)
        {
            _lastAppliedSyncAt = summary!.At;

            try
            {
                await State.ReloadFromStoreAsync();
                CheckPullRequestsAfterLoad();
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                Toasts.Publish(ToastMessage.Error($"The backlog could not be refreshed after a sync: {ex.Message}", TasksReloadErrorTestId));
            }
        }

        if (!InboxState.Loaded || !(applied || failed)) return;

        try
        {
            await InboxState.ReloadAsync();
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            Toasts.Publish(ToastMessage.Error($"The Inbox could not be refreshed after a sync: {ex.Message}", InboxReloadErrorTestId));
        }
    });

    /// <summary>The cycle whose applied changes the Tasks pane and the Inbox last
    /// reloaded for, so the other raise of that same cycle — and every raise for a
    /// cycle that applied nothing since — reloads neither a second time.</summary>
    private DateTimeOffset? _lastAppliedSyncAt;

    /// <summary>
    /// A task was written somewhere other than the Tasks pane — most often the
    /// MCP server an agent drives while the person watches the list — so the
    /// pane reloads from the store.
    /// <para>
    /// On the renderer's dispatcher, because the state raises this on the
    /// writer's thread and a reload replaces the rows the renderer reads. And
    /// coalesced through <see cref="CoalescedReload"/>, because an agent's burst is
    /// one signal per write. A popped-out window reloads its own list the same
    /// way. The reload keeps its own guard and waits out an open editor; see
    /// <c>TasksDesktopState.ReloadFromStoreAsync</c>.
    /// </para></summary>
    private void HandleTasksWrittenElsewhere() => _ = InvokeAsync(async () =>
    {
        try
        {
            await _tasksReload.RunAsync(async () =>
            {
                await State.ReloadFromStoreAsync();
                CheckPullRequestsAfterLoad();
            });
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            Toasts.Publish(ToastMessage.Error($"The backlog could not be refreshed after a change made outside it: {ex.Message}", TasksReloadErrorTestId));
        }
    });

    /// <summary>The reload <see cref="HandleTasksWrittenElsewhere"/> runs, and the
    /// one owed behind it. Only touched on the renderer's dispatcher.</summary>
    private readonly CoalescedReload _tasksReload = new();

    /// <summary>Where the two discarded reloads report a failure, for a test
    /// to find and a reader to read.</summary>
    private const string InboxReloadErrorTestId = "inbox-reload-error";
    private const string TasksReloadErrorTestId = "tasks-reload-error";

    /// <summary>
    /// Keeps the Devbook surface honest about what it can show, after the load and
    /// on every Tasks, Inbox or feature change. With its flag switched off, or
    /// nothing left for it to read — no Brain and no repository devbook — the
    /// Devbook leaves the screen for the workspace and is remembered so, as the
    /// side pane closed for the same reasons; it does not come back by itself when
    /// the reason goes. Then the scope is kept to what the screen can show.
    /// </summary>
    private void SynchronizeDevbookAvailability()
    {
        if (_takeover == DevbookSurfaceId && !DevbookPaneOptionVisible) CloseSurface();

        KeepRepositoryScopeSingleWhereOnlyOneFits();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The scope holds several repositories only while something on screen can show
    /// several — see <see cref="RepositoryScopeTakesSeveral"/>.
    /// <para>
    /// Several at once is a list affordance: the backlog list and the dashboard can
    /// show more than one repository, and Tools shows none. So when the list goes —
    /// left behind for a screen that cannot show several — the scope drops back to
    /// the anchor, the repository the Devbook reads. Called from every path that
    /// changes what is on screen: the view and
    /// surface switches call it themselves, and the restore and every state change
    /// through <see cref="SynchronizeDevbookAvailability"/>.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private void KeepRepositoryScopeSingleWhereOnlyOneFits()
    {
        if (!RepositoryScopeTakesSeveral) State.NarrowRepositoryScopeToAnchor();
    }

    /// <summary>Triage: the Inbox turned an item into backlog entries, and the
    /// task list reads them in, so they are there when the reader goes back to it.
    /// <para>
    /// Only the data moves: the Inbox has the screen, and leaving it for the list
    /// would take away the queue the reader is working through. The view under it
    /// stays what it was — the reader chose it, and the Inbox option returns to it.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The Tasks pane is reloaded from the store rather than told what changed.
    /// The Inbox's module wrote the entries through a port an adapter answers,
    /// so as far as the Tasks state is concerned somebody else wrote its
    /// database — which is exactly the case its store reload already handles.
    /// The shell knows both panes exist and nothing about what either holds.
    /// </para></summary>
    private void HandleInboxRouted(InboxRoutedDto routed) => RefreshTasksAfterRouting();

    /// <summary>The same, once for a whole batch: one import made every entry,
    /// so one reload shows them all.</summary>
    private void HandleInboxBatchRouted(InboxBatchRoutedDto batch) => RefreshTasksAfterRouting();

    /// <summary>An undone "Move to backlog" deleted the entries it made: the
    /// Tasks list reloads so they leave it, and no pane is opened for it.</summary>
    private void HandleInboxRouteUndone() => _ = InvokeAsync(async () =>
    {
        try
        {
            await State.ReloadFromStoreAsync();
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            Toasts.Publish(ToastMessage.Error($"The backlog could not be refreshed after the undo: {ex.Message}", TasksReloadErrorTestId));
        }

        StateHasChanged();
    });

    private void RefreshTasksAfterRouting() => _ = InvokeAsync(async () =>
    {
        try
        {
            await State.ReloadFromStoreAsync();
            CheckPullRequestsAfterLoad();
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            // The entries were made — the Inbox said so — and only the task
            // list failed to read them. Said on a toast, for the reason
            // HandleTaskSyncChanged gives: nothing awaits this.
            Toasts.Publish(ToastMessage.Error($"The backlog could not be refreshed after routing: {ex.Message}", TasksReloadErrorTestId));
        }

        StateHasChanged();
    });

    /// <summary>
    /// The repository's identity mark, as classes.
    /// <para>
    /// Which hue is the settings store's answer and not this screen's — every surface
    /// asks the same place, which is what stops the same project being two colours.
    /// The mark is additive: the chip keeps the surface, border and text it already
    /// had, and gains a rule down its leading edge. See
    /// <c>.devbook/design/color-scheme.md#band-identity-tokens</c>.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private string? RepositoryMarkClass(string alias) =>
        State.RepositoryColours.TryGetValue(alias, out var colour) ? $"repo-mark repo-mark--{colour}" : null;

    /// <summary>
    /// Shows or hides the repository identity hues across every surface.
    /// <para>
    /// The state class writes it, because the settings store is where the hue itself is
    /// answered and this is the same fact seen from the other side. The store's message
    /// on a failed write is dropped rather than shown: the choice has taken effect for
    /// this session either way, and the header has no room for a line of prose that
    /// would outlive the gesture it belongs to. Settings is where a settings file that
    /// cannot be written gets reported.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private void ShowRepositoryColours(bool visible) => _ = State.SetRepositoryColoursVisible(visible);

    /// <summary>
    /// A press on a scope chip.
    /// <para>
    /// The repository scope is global: it narrows the backlog list and, through the
    /// anchor handed to the pane, what knowledge is read beside it. A plain press is
    /// the single select the strip always had — this repository, or none when it was
    /// already alone in the scope. With Ctrl held (Cmd on a Mac) the press adds the
    /// repository beside the ones already scoped, or takes it back out: the
    /// convention every file manager has, so it needs no teaching beyond the tooltip.
    /// A plain press on a chip inside a wider scope narrows to that one, for the
    /// same reason — that is what a plain click on one of several selected files does.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The modifier counts only while what is on screen can show several — the task
    /// list, the Dashboard, or the session list; see <see cref="RepositoryScopeTakesSeveral"/>. Without
    /// one of those there is nothing a second repository could be shown in, so the
    /// press is an ordinary one.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private void ScopeRepository(string repositoryAlias, MouseEventArgs args)
    {
        if ((args.CtrlKey || args.MetaKey) && RepositoryScopeTakesSeveral)
        {
            State.ToggleRepositoryInScope(repositoryAlias);
            return;
        }

        var alone = State.SelectedRepositoryAliases is [var only]
            && string.Equals(only, repositoryAlias, StringComparison.Ordinal);
        State.SetRepositoryFilter(alone ? string.Empty : repositoryAlias);
    }

    /// <summary>Where the Brain folder is kept, or null in a host that composes none.
    /// Read for the chip's word: until a folder is set it reads "Set up your Brain".</summary>
    private IBrainSettings? _brainSettings;

    /// <summary>The Brain the Devbook pane reads, or null in a host that composes none.</summary>
    private IBrainFolderSource? _brainFolders;

    /// <summary>Where the Brain's task links are read, or null in a host that
    /// composes none — which then draws no Brain badge on any row.</summary>
    private IBrainTaskLinks? _brainTaskLinks;

    /// <summary>The owner's Brain notes by the task id they name, as last read.</summary>
    private IReadOnlyDictionary<string, IReadOnlyList<BrainTaskNote>> _brainNotesByTask =
        new Dictionary<string, IReadOnlyList<BrainTaskNote>>();

    /// <summary>Only the latest read lands.</summary>
    private int _brainNotesRead;

    /// <summary>
    /// Reads which Brain notes name which tasks. A Brain that cannot be read leaves
    /// the badges as they were: they are a convenience, and the next change reads
    /// again.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task ReadBrainNotesByTaskAsync()
    {
        if (_brainTaskLinks is null) return;

        var read = ++_brainNotesRead;
        IReadOnlyDictionary<string, IReadOnlyList<BrainTaskNote>> notes;
        try
        {
            notes = await _brainTaskLinks.NotesByTaskAsync();
        }
        catch (Exception exception) when (exception is IOException or UnauthorizedAccessException or InvalidOperationException or OperationCanceledException)
        {
            return;
        }

        if (read != _brainNotesRead) return;
        _brainNotesByTask = notes;
        await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
    }

    private async void HandleBrainFoldersChanged()
    {
        try
        {
            await InvokeAsync(ReadBrainNotesByTaskAsync);
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // An async void handler: a badge is not worth the process.
        }
    }

    private global::Backlog.Desktop.UI.Devbook.DevbookScenarioImageSources? _devbookImages;

    /// <summary>The image source for an entry's repository, which the Tasks pane
    /// cascades over the entry's body.</summary>
    private Backlog.UI.Components.Devbook.IDevbookImageSource? DevbookImagesForEntry(string? repositoryAlias) =>
        _devbookImages?.For(repositoryAlias);

    /// <summary>A repository's scenario catalog arrived or moved: the entry on
    /// screen draws its screenshots again.</summary>
    private async void HandleDevbookImagesChanged()
    {
        try
        {
            await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // An async void handler: a screenshot is not worth the process.
        }
    }

    /// <summary>A task row's Brain notes, by the row's stored id.</summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<Backlog.UI.Components.Brain.BrainNoteLink> BrainNotesForTask(string taskId) =>
        _brainNotesByTask.TryGetValue(taskId, out var notes)
            ? [.. notes.Select(note => new Backlog.UI.Components.Brain.BrainNoteLink(note.NotePath, note.Title))]
            : [];

    /// <summary>
    /// A Brain note at <paramref name="path"/> — a task row's badge, or a Brain search
    /// result, asked through the navigator: the Devbook on screen, on the Brain side,
    /// at the note, opened over the view, which waits underneath.
    /// </summary>
    private Task OpenBrainNoteAsync(string path)
    {
        // The badges are read whatever the Devbook's flag says, so a press can come
        // while there is no Devbook to open it in: said out loud, as an entry's
        // reference says it, rather than a press that does nothing.
        if (!IsFeatureEnabled(DevbookFeatures.RepositoryDevbook) || _brainFolders is null)
        {
            Toasts.Publish(ToastMessage.Warning(
                "The Devbook is not available here, so this Brain note cannot be opened.",
                DevbookUnavailableTestId));

            return Task.CompletedTask;
        }

        OpenBrainChapter(path);
        ShowDevbook();

        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    private void HandleBrainSettingsChanged() => _ = InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);

    /// <summary>The Brain chip's word: "Brain", or "Set up your Brain" while no folder
    /// is set — the words <see cref="BrainSetup"/> keeps for the chip and the Settings
    /// card alike.</summary>
    private string BrainChipLabel => _brainSettings is { IsSet: true } ? BrainSetup.Label : BrainSetup.SetUpLabel;

    /// <summary>
    /// A press on the Brain chip — <see cref="ScopeRepository"/>'s rules, for the Brain:
    /// a plain press picks it alone, or clears the scope when it already was alone; Ctrl
    /// adds it beside the repositories already pressed, or takes it back out, while a
    /// surface that can show several is on screen.
    /// <para>
    /// It scopes whether or not a folder is set (local ADR 0027, §1): the tasks filed
    /// against no repository are there either way. Its tooltip says where the folder is
    /// set; the Devbook pane on the Brain asks for one.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private void ScopeBrain(MouseEventArgs args)
    {
        if ((args.CtrlKey || args.MetaKey) && RepositoryScopeTakesSeveral)
        {
            State.ToggleBrainInScope();
            return;
        }

        if (State.SelectedRepositoryAliases is [var only] && BrainScope.Is(only))
        {
            State.SetRepositoryFilter(null);
            return;
        }

        State.SetBrainFilter();
    }

    /// <summary>The Brain's identity edge, always band 2, plus the anchor's class while
    /// there is a second chip for it to be distinct from.</summary>
    private static string BrainScopeChipClass(bool anchor) =>
        anchor ? "repo-mark repo-mark--2 app-header__brain-chip chip--anchor" : "repo-mark repo-mark--2 app-header__brain-chip";

    /// <summary>What the Brain chip does next, worded as a repository chip's is, and
    /// where its folder is set while none is.</summary>
    private string BrainScopeChipTitle(bool selected, bool anchor)
    {
        const string What = "Brain: the tasks filed against no repository";
        var setUp = _brainSettings is { IsSet: true } ? string.Empty : $" Set up your Brain folder in Settings › Storage.";

        string next;
        if (!RepositoryScopeTakesSeveral) next = selected ? $"Deselect {What}." : $"{What}.";
        else if (!selected) next = $"{What}. Ctrl+click to add it to the scope.";
        else if (State.SelectedRepositoryAliases.Count == 1) next = $"Deselect {What}.";
        else
        {
            var name = anchor ? $"{What} — Devbook follows this one" : What;
            next = $"{name}. Click to scope to it alone, Ctrl+click to remove it from the scope.";
        }

        return next + setUp;
    }

    /// <summary>The identity mark, plus the anchor's own class while there is a
    /// second chip for it to be distinct from, and the archived one while an archived
    /// repository is offered.</summary>
    private string? RepositoryScopeChipClass(GitHubRepositoryRef repository, bool anchor)
    {
        string?[] classes =
        [
            RepositoryMarkClass(repository.Alias),
            anchor ? "chip--anchor" : null,
            repository.Archived ? "chip--archived" : null
        ];

        var joined = string.Join(' ', classes.OfType<string>());
        return joined.Length == 0 ? null : joined;
    }

    /// <summary>What the chip does next, since the modifier is the one thing about
    /// the strip nothing on screen shows.</summary>
    private string RepositoryScopeChipTitle(GitHubRepositoryRef repository, bool selected, bool anchor) =>
        repository.Archived
            ? $"Archived. {ScopeChipTitle(repository, selected, anchor)}"
            : ScopeChipTitle(repository, selected, anchor);

    private string ScopeChipTitle(GitHubRepositoryRef repository, bool selected, bool anchor)
    {
        // Nothing to show several in, no modifier to name: the chip says what a
        // single select says.
        if (!RepositoryScopeTakesSeveral) return selected ? $"Deselect {repository.FullName}" : repository.FullName;

        if (!selected) return $"{repository.FullName}. Ctrl+click to add it to the scope";
        if (State.SelectedRepositoryAliases.Count == 1) return $"Deselect {repository.FullName}";

        var name = anchor ? $"{repository.FullName} — Devbook follows this one" : repository.FullName;
        return $"{name}. Click to scope to it alone, Ctrl+click to remove it from the scope";
    }

    // Asynchronous, because leaving the shell has interop calls to make on the
    // way out: the takeover Escape listener and the header fit hold this page, and
    // a Home that registered and never unregistered is a dead reference the next
    // Escape or resize calls into.
    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        CancelAsk();

        // Let go of what this page handed the Tasks views — unless the next page
        // mounted in this window has wired them already.
        if (_tasksViewWiring is { } wiring && ReferenceEquals(wiring.LinkedWork, _linkedWork)
            && wiring.BrainNotesFor?.Target == (object)this)
        {
            wiring.BrainNotesFor = null;
            wiring.DevbookImagesFor = null;
            wiring.LinkedWork = null;
        }
        if (_dashboardWiring is { } dashboard && dashboard.AskAi is not null && ReferenceEquals(dashboard.AskAi, _dashboardAskAi))
        {
            dashboard.AskAi = null;
        }
        State.Changed -= HandleStateChanged;
        if (_brainSettings is not null) _brainSettings.Changed -= HandleBrainSettingsChanged;
        if (_brainFolders is not null && _brainTaskLinks is not null) _brainFolders.Changed -= HandleBrainFoldersChanged;
        State.WrittenElsewhere -= HandleTasksWrittenElsewhere;
        State.PullRequestRecheckDue -= HandlePullRequestRecheckDue;
        InboxState.Changed -= HandleStateChanged;
        InboxState.Routed -= HandleInboxRouted;
        InboxState.BatchRouted -= HandleInboxBatchRouted;
        InboxState.RouteUndone -= HandleInboxRouteUndone;
        Features.Changed -= HandleFeaturesChanged;
        Navigator.ShowRequested -= HandleSurfaceRequested;
        if (_taskSync is not null) _taskSync.Changed -= HandleTaskSyncChanged;
        _notificationRequests?.Dispose();
        if (_devbookImages is not null)
        {
            _devbookImages.Changed -= HandleDevbookImagesChanged;
            _devbookImages.Dispose();
        }

        // Detached before anything else of this component goes, for the reason the
        // JS reference below is: the activator holds a delegate over this instance,
        // and a window that stays registered after it is gone is one the next
        // open_dashboard tries to render into.
        _surfaceAttachment?.Dispose();
        _surfaceAttachment = null;
        _mainWindowAttachment?.Dispose();
        _mainWindowAttachment = null;

        // Unregistered before the reference is disposed, so the map can never hold
        // one that is already dead. The other order leaves a window between the two
        // in which a resize finds a registration it cannot invoke.
        if (_selfReference is not null)
        {
            try
            {
                // The Escape listener's one slot is shared with any future Home, so
                // this would take a newer instance's registration out if disposal of
                // the old one ran after the new one registered. The router disposes
                // the outgoing page before rendering the incoming one, so it does
                // not — stated here so that a change to that ordering is read as
                // breaking this.
                await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("backlogTakeoverEscape.unregister");
                await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("backlogHeaderFit.disconnect", _header);
            }
            catch (JSException)
            {
            }
            catch (JSDisconnectedException)
            {
            }
            catch (InvalidOperationException)
            {
                // Disposed from a prerender: nothing was ever registered, so
                // there is nothing to unregister.
            }
        }

        _selfReference?.Dispose();
        _selfReference = null;
    }
}
